/* Math Quest Library: leveled stories 1-10 (levels 2-3 live in index.html). */
window.MORE_STORIES=[{"id":"r1_1","lv":1,"e":"🪁","t":"The Red Kite","p":["Sam has a red kite. He runs up the hill with Dad. But the air is still. The kite will not fly. Sam sits down in the grass. He is sad.","Then the wind blows hard. 'Run, Sam, run!' says Dad. Sam runs fast. The kite goes up, up, up! It flies over the trees. Sam smiles. 'We did not give up!' he says."],"q":[["What did Sam want to do?",["fly his kite","climb a tree","go back home","find a lost dog"],0,"The story says Sam has a red kite and runs up the hill to fly it."],["Why did the kite not fly at first?",["Sam ran too fast.","There was no wind.","The kite was broken.","Dad held the string."],1,"The story says 'the air is still,' so there was no wind."],["What is the lesson of the story?",["Kites are only for grown-ups.","Hills are too big to climb.","Keep trying and do not give up.","Stay inside when it is windy."],2,"Sam waited, tried again, and said, 'We did not give up!'"]]},{"id":"r1_2","lv":1,"e":"🦁","t":"The Lion and the Mouse","p":["A big lion was asleep. A mouse ran over his nose. The lion woke and grabbed the mouse. 'Please let me go,' said the mouse. 'One day I will help you.'","The lion laughed, but he let the mouse go.","Later, the lion got stuck in a net. The mouse came and chewed the ropes. The lion was free! Little friends can be big helpers."],"q":[["How did the mouse help the lion?",["It brought him food.","It woke him up.","It called for other lions.","It chewed the ropes of the net."],3,"The story says 'The mouse came and chewed the ropes.'"],["Why did the lion laugh at the mouse?",["He did not think a little mouse could help him.","The mouse told a funny joke.","The mouse tickled his nose.","He was happy to see a friend."],0,"The mouse said it would help one day, and the big lion laughed because the mouse was so small."],["What does the story teach?",["Lions are always mean.","Small friends can be big helpers.","Never take a nap.","Nets are good for lions."],1,"The last line says, 'Little friends can be big helpers.'"]]},{"id":"r1_3","lv":1,"e":"🐸","t":"From Egg to Frog","p":["A frog starts as a tiny egg in a pond. The egg hatches into a tadpole. A tadpole has a long tail. It swims like a fish and lives in the water.","Soon the tadpole grows back legs. Then it grows front legs. Its tail gets smaller and smaller. At last, it is a frog! Now it can hop on land and swim too."],"q":[["What does a frog start as?",["a tadpole","a fish","a tiny egg","a small frog"],2,"The first line says, 'A frog starts as a tiny egg in a pond.'"],["Which legs does a tadpole grow first?",["front legs","no legs","all four at once","back legs"],3,"The story says it grows back legs, and 'then it grows front legs.'"],["What happens to the tadpole's tail?",["It gets smaller and smaller.","It gets longer.","It turns into a leg.","It stays the same."],0,"The story says 'Its tail gets smaller and smaller.'"]]},{"id":"r1_4","lv":1,"e":"⛸️","t":"Mia on the Ice","p":["Mia goes to the ice rink. She wants to spin like a top. She tries to spin, but she falls down. Bump! Her face gets hot.","Coach Kim helps her up. 'Everyone falls,' she says. 'Try again.' Mia tries again and again. At the end of the day, she spins around three times. Mia cheers! Her coach cheers too."],"q":[["How did Mia feel when she fell?",["happy","embarrassed","sleepy","hungry"],1,"The story says 'Her face gets hot,' which shows she felt embarrassed."],["What did Coach Kim tell Mia?",["Go home and rest.","Skate faster.","Everyone falls, so try again.","Do not spin."],2,"Coach Kim said, 'Everyone falls. Try again.'"],["What can you tell about Mia?",["She does not like to skate.","She is afraid of her coach.","She gives up fast.","She keeps trying until she can do it."],3,"Mia 'tries again and again' until she can spin three times."]]},{"id":"r1_5","lv":1,"e":"🍰","t":"Where Is the Cake?","p":["Mom made a cake. She put it on the table. Then the phone rang. When Mom came back, the cake was gone!","Nina looked for clues. She saw crumbs on the floor. The crumbs made a line to the dog bed. Max the dog was in his bed. He had white frosting on his nose. 'Max!' said Nina. Max wagged his tail."],"q":[["Who took the cake?",["Max the dog","Nina","Mom","a cat"],0,"The crumbs led to Max, and he had frosting on his nose."],["Which clue was on Max's nose?",["crumbs","white frosting","mud","a bone"],1,"The story says Max 'had white frosting on his nose.'"],["How did the crumbs help Nina?",["They were good to eat.","They told her it was time for lunch.","They made a trail to the dog bed.","They showed where Mom went."],2,"The story says 'The crumbs made a line to the dog bed.'"]]},{"id":"r1_6","lv":1,"e":"🤝","t":"The New Girl","p":["Ana is new at school. At lunch, she sits all alone. She looks down at her tray.","Ben sees her. He picks up his tray and walks over. 'Can I sit here?' he asks. Ana smiles and nods. Ben shares his grapes. Ana shares her crackers. They talk and laugh. Now Ana has a friend."],"q":[["Why did Ana sit alone?",["She was mad at Ben.","She wanted to read a book.","She did not like lunch.","She was new and did not know anyone yet."],3,"The story says 'Ana is new at school,' so she did not have friends there yet."],["What did Ben do?",["He sat with Ana and shared his food.","He told a teacher.","He ate by himself.","He gave Ana his tray."],0,"Ben asked to sit with Ana and 'shares his grapes.'"],["What is the lesson of the story?",["Grapes are better than crackers.","Being kind can help someone feel welcome.","Always sit by yourself at lunch.","New kids should stay quiet."],1,"Ben was kind to Ana, and 'Now Ana has a friend.'"]]},{"id":"r4_1","lv":4,"e":"🐧","t":"Penguins in the Coldest Winter","p":["Antarctica in winter is one of the harshest places on Earth. The sun barely rises, temperatures can fall below minus 40 degrees, and icy winds howl across the frozen sea. Yet this is exactly when emperor penguins raise their chicks.","After the female lays a single egg, she carefully passes it to the male and heads to the ocean to feed. The male balances the egg on top of his feet and covers it with a warm fold of skin called a brood pouch. For about two months, he stands guard without eating a single meal.","How does he survive? Emperor penguins have thick layers of fat and tightly packed feathers that trap heat. But their greatest weapon against the cold is teamwork. Thousands of males crowd together in a huddle. The birds slowly shuffle so that each one takes a turn in the warm center and a turn on the chilly edge.","By the time the chick hatches, the female returns with a belly full of fish. Thanks to fat, feathers, and cooperation, the family has survived the coldest winter on the planet."],"q":[["What is this article mainly about?",["how emperor penguins survive Antarctic winters and raise their chicks","why penguins cannot fly","how female penguins catch fish","what Antarctica looks like in summer"],0,"Every paragraph explains how the penguins stay alive and care for their egg through the winter."],["What is a 'brood pouch'?",["a nest built from stones","a warm fold of skin that covers the egg","a hole dug in the ice","a bag the female uses to carry fish"],1,"The text says the male 'covers it with a warm fold of skin called a brood pouch.'"],["Why do the penguins in the huddle keep shuffling around?",["They are searching for food.","They are trying to find their mates.","So each bird gets a turn in the warm center","So they can walk to the ocean"],2,"The article says each one 'takes a turn in the warm center and a turn on the chilly edge.'"],["Why does the male penguin go about two months without eating?",["He is not hungry in the cold.","The female eats his share.","There are no fish in the ocean in winter.","He must stay and guard the egg on his feet."],3,"He balances the egg on his feet and 'stands guard without eating a single meal' until the female returns."]]},{"id":"r4_2","lv":4,"e":"🐜","t":"The Ant and the Grasshopper","p":["All summer long, Grasshopper lounged in the tall grass, fiddling tunes and napping in the sunshine. Nearby, a small ant marched back and forth, hauling kernels of corn to her nest one heavy load at a time.","'Why work so hard on such a beautiful day?' Grasshopper asked. 'Come sing with me!' Ant set down her load and wiped her brow. 'I am storing food for winter,' she said. 'You should do the same.' Grasshopper only laughed. 'Winter is ages away. There is plenty of food right now.'","But the days grew shorter, and the grass turned brown. One morning, frost sparkled on every leaf. Grasshopper searched everywhere, but there was nothing left to eat. Shivering and hungry, he knocked at Ant's door.","Ant looked at him for a long moment. Then she opened the door wider. 'Come in,' she said, 'but next summer, you will work beside me.' Grasshopper nodded gratefully. He had learned that it is wise to prepare today for what you will need tomorrow."],"q":[["Why did Ant work so hard all summer?",["She did not like music.","She was storing food for the winter.","Grasshopper asked her to help him.","Her nest was falling apart."],1,"Ant says, 'I am storing food for winter.'"],["What happened after the frost came?",["Grasshopper could find nothing to eat.","Ant ran out of corn.","Grasshopper moved to a warmer field.","Ant asked Grasshopper for help."],0,"The story says 'Grasshopper searched everywhere, but there was nothing left to eat.'"],["What does the word 'lounged' mean in the first sentence?",["worked hard","hid carefully","ran quickly","relaxed lazily"],3,"Grasshopper was 'fiddling tunes and napping in the sunshine,' which shows he was relaxing."],["What lesson does this fable teach?",["Music is a waste of time.","Never let anyone into your home.","It is wise to prepare now for what you will need later.","Summer is the best season of the year."],2,"The last sentence says 'it is wise to prepare today for what you will need tomorrow.'"]]},{"id":"r4_3","lv":4,"e":"✈️","t":"The Brothers Who Learned to Fly","p":["Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. Fixing bikes paid the bills, but the brothers had a much bigger dream. They wanted to build a machine that could carry a person through the air.","Many inventors had tried and failed. The Wrights studied birds and noticed that they tilted their wings to turn and keep their balance. The brothers tested gliders and even built a small wind tunnel to test hundreds of wing shapes. Each failure taught them something new.","They chose Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, for their tests because it had steady winds and soft sand for landings. On December 17, 1903, Orville lay on the lower wing of their engine-powered Flyer while Wilbur ran alongside. The machine lifted off and flew for 12 seconds, covering about 120 feet.","That first flight was shorter than the wingspan of a modern jumbo jet, but it changed history. Later that same day, Wilbur flew 852 feet in 59 seconds. The age of airplanes had begun."],"q":[["Why did the Wright brothers test their machine at Kitty Hawk?",["It was close to their shop in Dayton.","Crowds of people lived there to watch.","It had steady winds and soft sand.","It had the tallest hills in America."],2,"The text says Kitty Hawk 'had steady winds and soft sand for landings.'"],["What did the brothers learn by studying birds?",["Birds fly best in the morning.","Wings must flap very fast.","Birds need soft sand to land.","Birds tilt their wings to turn and balance."],3,"The article says birds 'tilted their wings to turn and keep their balance.'"],["What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?",["to explain how the Wright brothers made the first powered airplane flight","to convince readers to open a bicycle shop","to tell a funny made-up story about birds","to teach readers how to repair a bicycle"],0,"The passage gives facts about how the Wrights worked toward and made their 1903 flight."],["What does the sentence 'Each failure taught them something new' tell you about the brothers?",["They gave up easily.","They kept learning from mistakes and did not quit.","They were bad at building things.","They wanted other inventors to fail."],1,"Instead of quitting after failures, they used them to learn, and they finally flew."]]},{"id":"r4_4","lv":4,"e":"⛸️","t":"Nadia's Big Skate","p":["Nadia had practiced her figure skating program for months. Tomorrow was her first real competition, and her stomach felt as if it were full of fluttering moths.","At the rink, she laced her skates with shaking fingers. Her coach, Mr. Park, knelt beside her. 'Remember,' he said, 'the judges are watching your skating, not just your mistakes. If you fall, get up and keep telling the story of your music.'","The music began, and Nadia glided onto the ice. Her spiral was smooth and graceful. Her spin was fast and steady. Then came the hardest part, a jump called a double salchow. She leaped, turned twice in the air, and landed hard on the ice.","For a second, Nadia wanted to skate off and hide. Instead, she remembered Mr. Park's words. She sprang up, caught the beat of the music, and finished with her brightest smile.","Nadia didn't win a medal that day. But when she stepped off the ice, Mr. Park gave her a high five. 'That,' he said, 'was the bravest thing I've seen all season.'"],"q":[["How did Nadia feel the day before the competition?",["bored","angry","sleepy","nervous"],3,"Her stomach felt 'full of fluttering moths,' and later her fingers were shaking, which shows she was nervous."],["What does 'sprang up' mean in this story?",["fell down again","moved very slowly","jumped up quickly","called for help"],2,"After falling, Nadia 'sprang up' and caught the beat of the music, so she got up fast."],["Why did Mr. Park call Nadia's skate the bravest thing he had seen?",["She won a gold medal.","She got up after falling and finished strong.","She landed the jump perfectly.","She skated without any music."],1,"She fell on the jump but 'sprang up' and 'finished with her brightest smile.'"],["What did Nadia do right after she fell?",["She remembered her coach's words and got back up.","She skated off the ice to hide.","She did her spiral.","She laced her skates again."],0,"The story says she 'remembered Mr. Park's words. She sprang up.'"]]},{"id":"r4_5","lv":4,"e":"🍅","t":"The Case of the Vanishing Tomatoes","p":["Every morning that week, Leo found fewer ripe tomatoes in Grandpa's garden. On Monday there were ten. By Thursday there were only three, and one lay half-eaten in the dirt.","'I bet it's your sister,' Grandpa joked. But Leo knew that his sister, Rosa, hated tomatoes. He decided to investigate like a real detective.","First, he looked for clues. Near the fence, he found tiny tracks pressed into the soft mud. Each print looked like a little hand with five long fingers. He also noticed that the lid of the garbage can had been pushed open, and scraps were scattered on the ground.","Next, Leo thought about timing. The tomatoes always disappeared overnight, never during the day. Whoever the thief was, it came out after dark.","That night, Leo and Grandpa waited by the window with a flashlight. Around midnight, a masked face peeked over the fence. Leo grinned. 'Case closed,' he whispered. 'It's a raccoon!' The next day, they put a latch on the garbage can and a net over the tomatoes."],"q":[["Why didn't Leo believe his sister took the tomatoes?",["Rosa was away all week.","Rosa hates tomatoes.","Rosa was helping Grandpa.","Rosa was too short to reach them."],1,"The story says 'Leo knew that his sister, Rosa, hated tomatoes.'"],["Which clue told Leo the thief came out at night?",["The tracks were in the mud.","The garbage can lid was open.","One tomato was half-eaten.","The tomatoes always vanished overnight."],3,"Leo noticed 'The tomatoes always disappeared overnight, never during the day.'"],["Why did the 'masked face' tell Leo the thief was a raccoon?",["Raccoons have dark fur around their eyes that looks like a mask.","The face belonged to the neighbor's cat.","Grandpa had told him raccoons love tomatoes.","He saw the animal carrying a tomato."],0,"A raccoon's dark eye markings look like a mask, and this matched the hand-like tracks Leo found."],["Why did Leo and Grandpa add a latch and a net?",["to trap the raccoon in the garden","to keep Rosa out of the garden","to stop the raccoon from finding food there","to make the garden look nicer"],2,"The raccoon had been eating tomatoes and garbage scraps, so they covered both food sources."]]},{"id":"r4_6","lv":4,"e":"🌋","t":"The Cracked Volcano","p":["Owen and his best friend, Marcus, had spent two weeks building a model volcano for the science fair. It stood on Marcus's kitchen table, painted brown and orange, with a crater ready for a baking-soda eruption.","While Marcus went to find more vinegar, Owen leaned in to admire the paint. His elbow bumped the model. It slid off the table and hit the floor with a crack. A long split ran down one side.","Owen's heart pounded. Nobody had seen. He could set it back on the table, turn the crack toward the wall, and say nothing. Marcus might think it had cracked while drying.","But Owen pictured Marcus finding the crack at the fair, in front of everyone. His stomach twisted. When Marcus came back, Owen took a deep breath. 'I knocked it over,' he said. 'I'm really sorry.'","Marcus stared at the crack, then sighed. 'Thanks for telling me,' he said. 'Grab the glue. We still have two days.' Together they patched the volcano, and it erupted perfectly at the fair."],"q":[["What choice did Owen have to make?",["whether to enter the science fair","whether to paint the volcano orange","whether to tell the truth or hide what happened","whether to use vinegar or water"],2,"Owen thought he 'could set it back... and say nothing,' but he had to decide whether to be honest."],["What does 'admire' mean in the sentence 'Owen leaned in to admire the paint'?",["to look at with pleasure","to break apart","to hide from","to clean up"],0,"Owen leaned in to enjoy looking at the painted volcano they had made."],["Why did Owen decide to tell Marcus the truth?",["Marcus saw him drop the model.","His mom told him to confess.","He wanted a new project partner.","He imagined how bad Marcus would feel finding the crack at the fair."],3,"Owen 'pictured Marcus finding the crack at the fair, in front of everyone. His stomach twisted.'"],["What is the main lesson of this story?",["Science fairs are too stressful.","Telling the truth can keep a friendship strong.","It is better to work alone on projects.","Glue can fix any problem."],1,"Marcus thanked Owen for being honest, and they fixed the volcano together as friends."]]},{"id":"r5_1","lv":5,"e":"🐙","t":"Masters of Disguise","p":["Imagine a creature that can change color in less than a second, reshape its skin to look like rough coral, and squeeze its whole body through a hole the size of a coin. It is not a superhero from a comic book. It is the octopus, one of the most remarkable animals in the ocean.","An octopus's skin is packed with thousands of tiny color cells called chromatophores. Each cell is like a small, stretchy sac filled with pigment. When muscles pull the sac open, the color spreads out and becomes visible. When the muscles relax, the color shrinks back to a speck. By controlling thousands of these cells at once, an octopus can flash patterns, blend into sand, or turn a sudden, startling red.","Color is only part of the trick. Many octopuses can also raise small bumps on their skin, called papillae, to copy the texture of rocks and seaweed. Because an octopus has no bones, it can fold itself into cracks where predators cannot follow.","Why does an octopus need so many disguises? Its soft body makes it a tasty meal for sharks, eels, and seals. Camouflage helps it stay hidden while it rests, and it also helps the octopus sneak up on crabs and shrimp.","Scientists are still studying how octopuses decide which disguise to use. Some engineers are even trying to design materials that change color the way octopus skin does."],"q":[["What is the main idea of this article?",["Octopuses are the largest animals in the ocean.","Octopuses have no natural enemies.","Engineers have already built a color-changing robot octopus.","Octopuses have amazing body features that help them hide and hunt."],3,"Each paragraph explains a way the octopus disguises itself and why that helps it survive and catch food."],["Based on the article, what are 'chromatophores'?",["bumps that change the texture of the skin","tiny cells in the skin that hold color","the bones inside an octopus's arms","a kind of coral where octopuses hide"],1,"The text calls them 'tiny color cells called chromatophores.'"],["What happens when muscles pull a chromatophore open?",["The color spreads out and becomes visible.","The octopus grows larger.","The octopus squirts a cloud of ink.","The color shrinks back to a speck."],0,"The article says 'When muscles pull the sac open, the color spreads out and becomes visible.'"],["Why is an octopus able to squeeze into tiny cracks?",["Its skin can change color.","It has eight flexible arms.","It has no bones.","It can raise bumps called papillae."],2,"The text says 'Because an octopus has no bones, it can fold itself into cracks.'"]]},{"id":"r5_2","lv":5,"e":"🔬","t":"Marie Curie's Great Discovery","p":["Maria Sklodowska was born in Warsaw, Poland, in 1867. She loved learning, but the university in Warsaw did not accept women. Maria refused to let that stop her. For years she worked as a tutor and a governess, saving money and studying on her own in the evenings.","In 1891, she moved to Paris, France, to study at a famous university called the Sorbonne. There she used the French form of her name, Marie. She was often cold and hungry in her tiny apartment, but she earned top honors in physics and mathematics. In Paris she met Pierre Curie, a scientist who shared her passion for research, and they married in 1895.","Together, the Curies studied mysterious rays given off by certain minerals. Marie named this effect 'radioactivity.' After years of grinding, boiling, and testing tons of rock, they discovered two new elements. Marie named one polonium, after her beloved homeland, Poland. The other they called radium.","In 1903, Marie became the first woman to win a Nobel Prize, sharing the prize in physics with Pierre and another scientist, Henri Becquerel. In 1911, she won a second Nobel Prize, this time in chemistry. She was the first person ever to win two.","Marie Curie spent her whole life doing research, and today an element called curium is named in honor of her and Pierre. Her determination opened doors for generations of women in science."],"q":[["Why did Maria work as a tutor and governess for years?",["to save money for her education","because she disliked school","to learn to speak French","because Pierre asked her to"],0,"The text says she worked those jobs while 'saving money and studying on her own.'"],["Why did Marie name one element polonium?",["It was first found in Poland.","Pierre chose the name for her.","It glowed like the North Pole's lights.","to honor her homeland, Poland"],3,"The article says she named it polonium 'after her beloved homeland, Poland.'"],["What does 'determination' mean in the final paragraph?",["good luck","great wealth","a firm refusal to give up","quiet shyness"],2,"Marie kept going even when the university would not accept women and when she was cold and hungry, which shows she refused to give up."],["What made Marie's 1911 Nobel Prize especially historic?",["It was the first Nobel Prize ever given in physics.","It made her the first person to win two Nobel Prizes.","She shared it with Pierre and Henri Becquerel.","It was awarded to her in Poland."],1,"The passage says 'She was the first person ever to win two.'"]]},{"id":"r5_3","lv":5,"e":"☀️","t":"The Wind and the Sun","p":["High above the hills, the North Wind and the Sun were having an argument. 'I am far stronger than you,' the Wind boasted, puffing out his cheeks. 'I can bend the tallest oak and whip the sea into mountains of foam.' The Sun only smiled. 'Strength comes in many forms,' she replied. 'Shall we test it?'","Just then, a traveler came walking along the road, wrapped in a heavy wool cloak. 'Whoever can make that man take off his cloak,' said the Sun, 'will be the stronger one.' The Wind agreed at once, certain of an easy victory.","He went first. He blew a cold, howling blast that sent leaves spinning and made the traveler stagger. But the harder the Wind blew, the more tightly the man clutched his cloak around his shoulders. The Wind roared until he was exhausted, yet the cloak stayed firmly in place.","Then it was the Sun's turn. She slipped out from behind a cloud and simply shone, gently and steadily. The air grew warm. The traveler loosened his grip, then unfastened the clasp at his neck. Before long, he pulled off the cloak, draped it over his arm, and rested in the shade of a tree.","The Wind sank into a sulky silence. He had learned what the Sun already knew: gentleness and warmth can often accomplish what force and bullying cannot."],"q":[["What contest did the Wind and the Sun agree to?",["to see who could blow down an oak tree","to see who could make the traveler take off his cloak","to see who could make the traveler walk faster","to see who could reach the traveler first"],1,"The Sun said, 'Whoever can make that man take off his cloak... will be the stronger one.'"],["What happened when the Wind blew harder and harder?",["The cloak flew off the traveler.","The traveler ran to find shelter.","The traveler held his cloak more tightly.","The Sun hid behind a cloud."],2,"The story says 'the harder the Wind blew, the more tightly the man clutched his cloak.'"],["What does the word 'boasted' mean in the first paragraph?",["whispered softly","asked politely","complained sadly","bragged proudly"],3,"The Wind was praising his own strength, saying 'I am far stronger than you.'"],["What lesson does this fable teach?",["Gentleness and warmth can succeed where force fails.","The loudest one always wins.","Always carry a cloak when you travel.","Never argue with your friends."],0,"The last line says 'gentleness and warmth can often accomplish what force and bullying cannot.'"]]},{"id":"r5_4","lv":5,"e":"🏀","t":"The Assist","p":["Jamal was the best shooter on the Riverside Hawks, and everybody knew it, especially Jamal. Whenever he got the ball, he launched a shot, no matter how many defenders surrounded him. Sometimes the ball swished through the net. Lately, it more often clanged off the rim.","After a close loss, Coach Rivera asked him to stay behind. She didn't scold him. Instead, she handed him a sheet of numbers from the game. 'You took twenty-two shots,' she said. 'Your teammates took eighteen, combined. Look at who was open.' Again and again, Priya or Tomás had been standing alone near the basket while Jamal forced shots through a crowd.","At the next practice, Coach Rivera ran a drill in which no one could shoot until the ball had been passed three times. Jamal grumbled at first. But as the team zipped the ball around, he noticed something surprising: the defense couldn't keep up. Every few passes, someone ended up wide open.","In the championship game, the score was tied with ten seconds left. Jamal dribbled toward the basket, and two defenders rushed at him. Out of the corner of his eye, he spotted Tomás alone under the hoop. The old Jamal would have shot anyway. Instead, he bounced a crisp pass to Tomás, who laid the ball in as the buzzer sounded.","His teammates mobbed Tomás, then hoisted Jamal onto their shoulders too. For the first time, Jamal understood that the best play isn't always the one that makes you the hero."],"q":[["What was Jamal's problem at the beginning of the story?",["He could not dribble well.","He was often late to practice.","He took too many shots instead of passing to open teammates.","He did not get along with Coach Rivera."],2,"He 'launched a shot, no matter how many defenders surrounded him,' while teammates stood open."],["Why did Coach Rivera most likely show Jamal the numbers instead of scolding him?",["She wanted him to see the problem for himself.","She planned to remove him from the team.","She was too tired to talk after the game.","She wanted him to count how many fans came."],0,"She said 'Look at who was open,' guiding Jamal to notice on his own that teammates were free."],["What did the passing drill help Jamal discover?",["Passing tires out your own team.","Quick passing makes it hard for the defense to keep up, creating open shots.","Only the best shooter should take shots.","Practices matter more than games."],1,"He noticed 'the defense couldn't keep up. Every few passes, someone ended up wide open.'"],["What is the theme of this story?",["Winning is the only thing that matters.","The best players never need to pass.","Coaches should be strict with their stars.","Helping the team succeed matters more than being the star."],3,"Jamal learned 'the best play isn't always the one that makes you the hero.'"]]},{"id":"r5_5","lv":5,"e":"🐹","t":"The Case of the Missing Hamster","p":["On Monday morning, Ms. Ortiz's fifth-grade classroom buzzed with alarm. The door of the hamster cage hung open, and Pepper, the class hamster, was nowhere to be found. Kids peered under desks and inside cubbies, but there was no sign of the small golden furball.","Jada pulled out her notebook. Instead of searching randomly, she decided to think like a hamster. She had read that hamsters are most active at night, love to collect food, and build nests in warm, dark, soft places. Pepper had probably been exploring all weekend.","First, she examined the cage. The latch was bent, which explained the escape. Next, she spotted a few empty sunflower seed shells on the floor, leading away from the cage toward the back of the room. Near the bookshelf, the trail ended, but Jada noticed something odd: a small pile of shredded tissue beside the reading corner.","She scanned the reading corner. There were hard wooden chairs, a metal book cart, and a large, squishy beanbag that sat right next to the heater. Jada knelt down and found a tiny hole in the beanbag's seam, just big enough for a hamster.","'She's in here,' Jada announced. Ms. Ortiz gently unzipped the cover. Curled up in a nest of tissue and stuffing, with cheeks packed full of seeds, lay Pepper, sound asleep.","That afternoon, the class repaired the latch. And Jada earned a new nickname: Detective Jada."],"q":[["How did Pepper escape from her cage?",["Someone left the classroom door open.","A student took her home for the weekend.","Pepper chewed through the bars.","The cage latch was bent."],3,"The story says 'The latch was bent, which explained the escape.'"],["Why did Jada decide to 'think like a hamster'?",["Knowing hamster habits would help her figure out where Pepper would hide.","She wanted to make her classmates laugh.","Ms. Ortiz told her to pretend.","She wanted to find the sunflower seeds."],0,"She used what she knew about hamsters liking 'warm, dark, soft places' to guide her search."],["Which clues best led Jada to the beanbag?",["the open cage door and the empty desks","the hard wooden chairs and the metal book cart","the shredded tissue nearby and the warm, soft spot by the heater","the notebook and the golden fur"],2,"The tissue was by the reading corner, and the beanbag was soft and 'right next to the heater,' just what a nesting hamster wants."],["What does 'scanned' mean in the sentence 'She scanned the reading corner'?",["cleaned carefully","looked over closely, searching","left in a hurry","drew a picture of"],1,"Jada was searching the area, noticing the chairs, cart, and beanbag one by one."]]},{"id":"r5_6","lv":5,"e":"🏅","t":"Second Place","p":["For three weeks, Aiden had worked on his science fair project: a water filter built from sand, gravel, and charcoal inside a cut-open plastic bottle. He tested it again and again, pouring muddy water in the top and watching clearer water drip out the bottom. He was sure it would win first place.","His best friend, Lily, had built a small wind turbine that lit up a string of tiny bulbs. When the judges announced the winners, Lily's name was called for first place. Aiden's was called for second.","Aiden clapped, but his hands felt stiff. A hot, prickly feeling crept up his neck. Why her and not me? he wondered. For the rest of the afternoon, he avoided Lily's table and pretended to be busy packing up his poster.","That evening, Aiden's grandmother found him sulking on the porch steps. He told her everything. She listened quietly, then asked, 'When Lily's bulbs lit up, how did you feel, before the judges said anything?' Aiden thought about it. 'Honestly? I thought it was really cool.'","'Then maybe,' Grandma said, 'you can be disappointed for yourself and proud of your friend at the same time. Both feelings can fit in one heart.'","The next morning, Aiden found Lily by the bike racks. 'Your turbine was amazing,' he said, and he meant it. Lily's face broke into a grin. 'Your filter was great too. Want to build something together next year?'"],"q":[["What feelings does the 'hot, prickly feeling' on Aiden's neck show?",["jealousy and disappointment","fear of the judges","excitement about winning","sickness from a cold"],0,"It came right after Lily won first place, and he wondered, 'Why her and not me?'"],["Why did Aiden avoid Lily's table for the rest of the afternoon?",["He was busy helping the judges.","Lily asked him to stay away.","He was upset that she had won instead of him.","He had to leave the fair early."],2,"He avoided her right after thinking, 'Why her and not me?' and 'pretended to be busy.'"],["What did Grandma mean by 'Both feelings can fit in one heart'?",["Aiden should hide how he feels.","A person can feel disappointed and proud of a friend at the same time.","Aiden should ask the judges to change their decision.","Winning is the only thing that matters."],1,"She said he could be 'disappointed for yourself and proud of your friend at the same time.'"],["How did Aiden change by the end of the story?",["He decided never to enter a science fair again.","He asked Lily to trade ribbons with him.","He stayed angry at Lily.","He honestly congratulated Lily, and their friendship grew stronger."],3,"He told Lily 'Your turbine was amazing,' 'and he meant it,' and she invited him to build something together."]]},{"id":"r6_1","lv":6,"e":"🧪","t":"The Perfect Graph","p":["Marcus and Theo had been best friends since second grade, so when the science fair came around, they decided to work as partners. Their question was simple: does music help bean plants grow faster? They set up six pots on Theo's windowsill, three with a small speaker playing classical music and three in silence.","For two weeks, Marcus measured the plants every afternoon and wrote the numbers in a notebook. The results were disappointing. The plants with music grew about the same as the plants without it. One of the music plants had even drooped and turned yellow.","The night before the fair, Theo sent Marcus a photo of their finished poster. Marcus stared at his screen. The graph showed the music plants towering over the silent ones, nearly twice as tall. None of those numbers were in the notebook.","'Where did these numbers come from?' Marcus texted.","'Relax,' Theo wrote back. 'Nobody wants to see a boring result. Judges like exciting projects. Besides, the music plants probably would have grown taller if that one hadn't gotten sick.'","Marcus put his phone down and looked at the notebook on his desk. He thought about Ms. Ortiz, who always said that a surprising 'no' was still a real answer. He also thought about Theo's face if he refused to go along. Theo had already printed the poster. He had worked on it all weekend.","In the morning, Marcus arrived early and found Theo taping the poster to their table. He took a deep breath and opened the notebook. 'I'm not trying to ruin anything,' he said. 'But if a judge asks to see our data, what do we show them? Let's put the real graph up and explain why our result matters anyway. Maybe music just doesn't do what people think it does.' Theo was quiet for a long moment. Then, slowly, he reached up and began peeling back the first corner."],"q":[["What is the main problem Marcus faces in the story?",["He cannot get the bean plants to grow at all.","He must decide whether to go along with made-up results to protect his friendship.","He forgot to measure the plants for the last week.","He wants to switch partners before the science fair."],1,"Theo's graph shows numbers that 'were not in the notebook,' and Marcus worries about 'Theo's face if he refused to go along.'"],["Why did Theo most likely change the numbers on the graph?",["He accidentally copied the wrong notebook.","Ms. Ortiz told him the project needed bigger numbers.","He believed an exciting result would impress the judges more than the true one.","He wanted Marcus to get in trouble."],2,"Theo says, 'Nobody wants to see a boring result. Judges like exciting projects.'"],["What does Ms. Ortiz mean when she says 'a surprising no was still a real answer'?",["An experiment that disproves an idea still teaches something true.","Students should always say no when asked to help.","Experiments that fail should be done again until they work.","Judges prefer projects with surprising titles."],0,"Marcus remembers this while deciding to 'explain why our result matters anyway.'"],["What does the ending suggest Theo is about to do?",["Leave the fair and go home.","Tape the fake graph up more firmly.","Ask a judge to decide who is right.","Take down the poster so they can show the real data."],3,"After a long pause, Theo \"began peeling back the first corner\" of the fake poster."]]},{"id":"r6_2","lv":6,"e":"🐺","t":"When the Wolves Came Back","p":["For about seventy years, no wolves howled in Yellowstone National Park. By the 1920s, people had hunted and trapped them until they disappeared from the park completely. At the time, many ranchers and park officials thought this was a good thing. Wolves hunted elk and sometimes killed livestock, so removing them seemed to make the land safer.","But an ecosystem is like a web, where every strand connects to others. Pull one strand, and the whole web can sag. Without wolves, the elk population grew large. Herds spent long hours near rivers and streams, nibbling young willow and aspen trees down to stubs. With fewer trees along the banks, songbirds lost nesting places, and beavers, which depend on willows for food and dam building, became scarce.","In 1995 and 1996, scientists brought gray wolves from Canada and released them in Yellowstone. The goal was to return a missing piece to the park. The wolves settled in quickly, formed packs, and began hunting elk once again.","Researchers soon noticed changes. Elk numbers dropped, and the elk that remained seemed to move around more instead of lingering in open valleys. In some areas, willows along the streams began growing taller. Beaver colonies increased, and their dams created ponds that became homes for fish, frogs, and ducks. Scavengers such as ravens, eagles, and even bears fed on the leftovers from wolf kills.","Scientists still debate how much of this change was caused by wolves alone. Harsh winters, human hunting outside the park, and growing numbers of bears also affected the elk. Nature is rarely as simple as one cause leading to one effect. Still, most researchers agree that the return of the wolves helped show something important. A top predator does more than eat its prey. It can shape the lives of plants and animals that it never even touches."],"q":[["What is the central idea of this article?",["Wolves are dangerous and should be kept away from people.","Removing or returning a top predator can affect an entire ecosystem.","Elk are the most important animals in Yellowstone.","Beavers were brought to Yellowstone from Canada in 1995."],1,"The article ends by saying a top predator 'can shape the lives of plants and animals that it never even touches.'"],["According to the article, why did willow trees shrink when the wolves were gone?",["Beavers cut them all down for dams.","Harsh winters froze their roots.","Large elk herds stayed near streams and ate the young trees.","Ranchers cleared them to make room for cattle."],2,"Without wolves, elk herds 'spent long hours near rivers and streams, nibbling young willow and aspen trees down to stubs.'"],["Why does the author compare an ecosystem to a web?",["To show that changing one part can affect many other parts.","To explain how spiders help control insects.","To describe the shape of rivers in the park.","To prove that wolves build dens like webs."],0,"The author writes, 'Pull one strand, and the whole web can sag.'"],["Which statement best shows the author's view about the evidence?",["Wolves were the only reason the elk population fell.","Scientists have proven wolves harm the park.","Bears caused all of the changes in Yellowstone.","The wolves mattered, but other causes also played a part."],3,"The author notes that 'harsh winters, human hunting outside the park, and growing numbers of bears also affected the elk.'"]]},{"id":"r6_3","lv":6,"e":"🦴","t":"Mary Anning, Fossil Hunter","p":["In the early 1800s, the cliffs near the town of Lyme Regis in southern England were always crumbling. Storms and waves chewed at the rock, and after each landslide, strange stony shapes appeared on the beach. A girl named Mary Anning, born there in 1799, grew up searching for them.","Mary's family was poor. Her father, a cabinetmaker, collected fossils and sold them to tourists, and he taught his children to do the same. After he died when Mary was about eleven, the family depended even more on what they could find. Fossil hunting was risky work. Mary climbed along slippery rocks and had to stay alert for falling cliffs.","When Mary was about twelve, her brother Joseph found a large skull in the cliffs. Over the following months, Mary carefully uncovered the rest of the skeleton. It belonged to an ichthyosaur, a sea reptile that had lived long before dinosaurs vanished. Scientists in London studied it with great excitement.","As she grew older, Mary made more important discoveries, including one of the first nearly complete plesiosaur skeletons, a creature with a small head and a very long neck. She taught herself geology and anatomy by reading and by dissecting modern animals, and experts traveled to Lyme Regis to learn from her.","Yet Mary received little public credit during her life. Women were not allowed to join the Geological Society of London, and men who bought her fossils often described them in scientific papers without mentioning her name. Mary once wrote that the world had used her badly.","Today, however, Mary Anning is remembered as one of the most important early fossil hunters. Her finds helped scientists understand that many kinds of animals had lived long ago and later disappeared forever, an idea that was still new and surprising in her time."],"q":[["What is the main purpose of this passage?",["To explain how to dig for fossils safely.","To describe the geography of southern England.","To tell about Mary Anning's discoveries and the challenges she faced.","To argue that plesiosaurs were more important than ichthyosaurs."],2,"The passage follows her finds and notes she 'received little public credit during her life.'"],["Why did Mary's family depend on selling fossils?",["They were poor, and her father's death left them with even less income.","Scientists paid them a regular salary.","Mary's mother ran a museum in London.","Fossils were the only thing tourists wanted to buy."],0,"The family was poor, and 'after he died... the family depended even more on what they could find.'"],["Why did Mary receive little credit during her lifetime?",["She kept her discoveries a secret.","Most of her fossils turned out to be fakes.","She never learned to read or write.","Women were shut out of scientific groups, and buyers often left her name out."],3,"Women 'were not allowed to join the Geological Society,' and men described her fossils 'without mentioning her name.'"],["Based on the passage, how did Mary's work affect science?",["It proved that dinosaurs still lived in the sea.","It helped show that many ancient animals had died out forever.","It led to new rules for building cabinets.","It showed that cliffs never change over time."],1,"Her finds helped scientists understand that animals 'had lived long ago and later disappeared forever.'"]]},{"id":"r6_4","lv":6,"e":"🔍","t":"The Case of the Missing Medal","p":["On Monday morning, the gold medal from the district spelling bee was gone from the glass case outside Mr. Barrett's classroom. The case had been locked Friday afternoon. By Monday it was still locked, but the medal and its blue ribbon had vanished.","'Only three people have a key,' said Mr. Barrett. 'Me, the custodian, Mrs. Lin, and the principal.' He looked worried. The medal belonged to the whole school, and the district ceremony was on Wednesday.","Nadia, who had won the medal, asked if she could help investigate. Mr. Barrett agreed. She started by looking closely at the case. There were no scratches on the lock, and the glass was not broken. Inside, where the medal had hung, she noticed a thin layer of dust on the shelf, except for a small clean rectangle near the back. 'Something was sitting here,' Nadia murmured. 'Something about the size of a box.'","Next she talked to Mrs. Lin, who said she had waxed the hallway floors on Saturday. 'I moved everything that was low to the ground,' Mrs. Lin said, 'but I never opened the case.' Then Nadia visited the principal, Dr. Hale. On her desk sat a stack of new display boxes with velvet lining.","'Oh, those,' Dr. Hale said. 'I ordered them so every award could have a proper box. I've been meaning to switch them all out.'","Nadia thought about the clean rectangle. The medal had not simply hung in the case. It had been sitting in its old cardboard box. Someone with a key had removed the box on purpose, not in a hurry, and had not broken anything. 'Dr. Hale,' Nadia asked politely, 'did you already start switching the medals into the new boxes?'","Dr. Hale blinked, then opened her bottom drawer. There, inside a velvet box, lay the gold medal and its blue ribbon. 'I completely forgot I started on Friday,' she laughed. 'Nice detective work.'"],"q":[["Why was the clean rectangle on the shelf an important clue?",["It proved the glass had been broken.","It showed that an object like a box had recently been taken away.","It showed that Mrs. Lin had cleaned inside the case.","It proved the medal had fallen behind the shelf."],1,"Nadia says, 'Something was sitting here... Something about the size of a box,' since dust had not settled there."],["What does the lack of scratches on the lock suggest?",["The medal was never in the case.","The thief was a student from another school.","Whoever took the medal used a key.","The lock had been replaced over the weekend."],2,"With no damage and 'only three people' holding keys, the person must have opened it normally."],["Which detail best helped Nadia connect the principal to the missing medal?",["Dr. Hale had new display boxes and planned to switch out the awards.","Dr. Hale was the one who ordered the district ceremony.","Mrs. Lin waxed the hallway floors on Saturday.","Mr. Barrett looked worried about Wednesday."],0,"Dr. Hale says she ordered new boxes and had 'been meaning to switch them all out.'"],["What word best describes how the mystery was solved?",["By luck, when the medal turned up on the floor.","By a confession from the custodian.","By Mr. Barrett checking a security camera.","By careful observation and reasoning."],3,"Nadia studied the case, questioned people and linked the clean rectangle to the new boxes."]]},{"id":"r6_5","lv":6,"e":"⛸️","t":"One and a Half Turns","p":["The rink was nearly empty at six in the morning, and the ice still gleamed from the resurfacing machine. Lucia skated slow circles to warm up, her breath puffing in small clouds. Today, like every day for the past three months, she was working on her axel.","The axel is the only jump in figure skating that takes off going forward. Because of that, a skater must spin one and a half times in the air instead of just once. For Lucia, the extra half turn felt enormous. She would glide forward, swing her free leg up, spring into the air, and then land crooked, crashing onto her hip.","'Again,' said Coach Petrov from the boards. He never raised his voice. He simply said 'again' and waited.","Lucia fell eleven times that morning. By the ninth fall, her eyes stung, and she was not sure if it was from the cold. She skated over to the boards. 'Maybe I'm just not an axel person,' she said.","Coach Petrov handed her a water bottle. 'Do you remember your first single jump?' he asked. Lucia nodded. It had taken her a whole summer. 'You fell then too. Your body is learning something your brain does not understand yet. Each fall teaches it a little.' Then he pointed at her arms. 'You are pulling in too late. Wrap them tighter as soon as you leave the ice, and you will turn faster.'","Lucia pushed off again. She glided forward, stepped up, and snapped her arms against her chest the instant her blade left the ice. The rink blurred. Then, with a sharp scrape, her right blade caught the ice and held. She was standing, arms spread wide, gliding backward.","For a moment she could not speak. Coach Petrov only nodded. 'Good,' he said. 'Now do it again.'"],"q":[["What is the theme of this story?",["Talent matters more than hard work.","Skaters should avoid jumps that are too difficult.","Progress often comes through repeated failure and patient practice.","Coaches should always praise their students loudly."],2,"Coach Petrov says, 'Each fall teaches it a little,' and Lucia lands the jump after many falls."],["According to the story, why is the axel harder than other jumps?",["It is performed only at the end of a program.","It must be done on one foot without any speed.","The skater must land facing forward.","It takes off forward, so the skater needs an extra half turn."],3,"The story explains the skater 'must spin one and a half times in the air instead of just once.'"],["Why does Coach Petrov remind Lucia of her first single jump?",["To show her that she has struggled before and still succeeded.","To suggest she should go back to easier jumps.","To tease her about how slowly she learned.","To explain that single jumps are more important than axels."],0,"It had taken her 'a whole summer,' and he says, 'You fell then too.'"],["What change finally helped Lucia land the jump?",["She took off backward instead of forward.","She pulled her arms in tightly right as she left the ice.","She skated faster than ever before.","She waited until the rink was empty."],1,"Coach Petrov told her to wrap her arms tighter 'as soon as you leave the ice,' and she 'snapped her arms against her chest.'"]]},{"id":"r6_6","lv":6,"e":"🕸️","t":"Arachne's Challenge","p":["Long ago in ancient Greece, a young woman named Arachne lived in a small village. Her father dyed wool purple, and Arachne spun it into thread and wove it into cloth. Her weaving was so fine that nymphs came from the forests and rivers just to watch her fingers fly across the loom.","People often told her, 'Surely you were taught by Athena herself!' Athena was the goddess of wisdom and crafts, including weaving. But Arachne, who wanted all the credit for herself, tossed her head at such praise. 'No one taught me,' she said. 'If Athena wants to compete with me, let her come. I am not afraid.'","One day an old woman with a walking stick appeared at Arachne's door. 'Child,' she said gently, 'be proud of your skill among mortals, but ask the goddess to forgive your words.' Arachne only laughed. Then the old woman straightened, and her disguise fell away. It was Athena.","The contest began at once. Athena wove a tapestry showing the gods in their glory, with small scenes in the corners warning of mortals punished for their pride. Arachne wove scenes of the gods behaving badly, playing tricks and causing trouble for humans. Her work was flawless. Not even Athena could find a single mistake in it.","But flawless work did not soften the goddess's anger at the insult. Athena tore the tapestry and struck Arachne with her shuttle. Then, feeling a touch of pity, she sprinkled Arachne with the juice of a magic herb. Arachne's hair fell away, her body shrank, and long thin legs sprouted from her sides. She had become a spider.","Ever since, the Greeks said, Arachne and her children have gone on spinning and weaving. Even today, scientists call spiders arachnids, an echo of the proud weaver who dared to challenge a goddess."],"q":[["What lesson does this myth most likely teach?",["Hard work always earns a reward from the gods.","Too much pride can lead to downfall, even for someone truly skilled.","People should never learn to weave.","Old people should not give advice to the young."],1,"Arachne boasts she is 'not afraid' of Athena and is punished despite work with no 'single mistake.'"],["Why did Athena visit Arachne disguised as an old woman?",["To buy some of Arachne's purple cloth.","To learn weaving secrets from her.","To give Arachne a chance to take back her boastful words.","To trick Arachne into leaving the village."],2,"The old woman urges her to 'ask the goddess to forgive your words.'"],["What do the corner scenes in Athena's tapestry suggest about her purpose?",["She was warning Arachne about what happens to proud mortals.","She wanted to show that gods behave badly.","She was trying to copy Arachne's design.","She wanted to make the weaving look longer."],0,"The corners showed 'mortals punished for their pride.'"],["How does the last paragraph connect the myth to the present?",["It explains why spiders are afraid of people.","It proves the story really happened.","It describes how modern weavers use spider silk.","It points out that the scientific word 'arachnid' comes from Arachne's name."],3,"It says scientists 'call spiders arachnids, an echo of the proud weaver.'"]]},{"id":"r7_1","lv":7,"e":"🎻","t":"The Message Jaya Forgot","p":["When Mr. Kim posted the results of the orchestra audition, Jaya read her name three times before she believed it. She had won the violin solo for the spring concert, the one piece everyone in the audience would remember. Her chest felt like a balloon about to lift off the floor. Then she noticed the second line on the list. Next to Rosa's name, in small letters, it said 'Did not audition.'","Jaya's balloon began to sink. Rosa was her stand partner and the strongest player in the section. Everyone assumed the solo would be hers. And Jaya suddenly remembered why it wasn't. On Tuesday, Mr. Kim had asked her to tell Rosa that auditions had moved from Thursday to Wednesday. Jaya had meant to text her that night, but between homework and dinner and a long phone call with her cousin, the message had slipped through her fingers like water.","At lunch, Rosa sat down across from her with a tired smile. 'I can't believe I got the day wrong,' she said. 'I must have written it down badly. Congratulations, though. Seriously. You'll be great.'","Jaya stirred her soup and said nothing. The truth sat in her throat like a stone. If she told Mr. Kim, he might hold another audition, and Jaya knew exactly how that would end. Rosa's tone was warm and effortless, while Jaya still had to fight for every high note. On the other hand, nobody would ever find out if she stayed quiet. Rosa already blamed herself. That was the part Jaya could not swallow. Rosa was apologizing for a mistake that wasn't hers.","After school, Jaya found Mr. Kim stacking music stands in the band room. Her hands were cold. 'I need to tell you something about the audition,' she said, and she explained everything, the forgotten text, the Wednesday change, all of it.","Mr. Kim listened without interrupting. When she finished, he rubbed his forehead. 'That took courage,' he said quietly. 'I'll let Rosa audition tomorrow. And Jaya, you played well on Wednesday. Whatever happens, that was real.'","Walking home, Jaya realized she felt lighter than she had all day, even though she had probably just given the solo away. The stone in her throat was gone. In its place was something steadier, something that felt a lot like respect for herself."],"q":[["What is the central conflict of the story?",["Jaya is jealous that Rosa is a stronger violinist.","Jaya must decide whether to admit a mistake that cost Rosa her audition.","Mr. Kim refuses to change the audition schedule.","Rosa accuses Jaya of lying about the audition date."],1,"Jaya remembers she forgot to pass along the date change, and 'nobody would ever find out if she stayed quiet.'"],["What does the simile 'the truth sat in her throat like a stone' suggest?",["Jaya has a sore throat from the cold.","Jaya is too angry to speak to Rosa.","Jaya is about to cry with happiness.","Keeping the secret feels heavy and uncomfortable to Jaya."],3,"The 'stone' is gone only after she confesses and feels 'lighter.'"],["Which detail most strongly pushes Jaya to tell the truth?",["Rosa blames herself for a mistake that was really Jaya's.","Mr. Kim asks Jaya directly whether she spoke to Rosa.","Jaya's cousin tells her she should confess.","Jaya decides she does not want the solo after all."],0,"The story says, 'That was the part Jaya could not swallow. Rosa was apologizing for a mistake that wasn't hers.'"],["How does the balloon image change over the course of the story?",["It stays the same, showing Jaya never doubts herself.","It pops, showing Jaya gives up playing violin.","It rises and then sinks, showing Jaya's joy turning to worry.","It is given to Rosa, showing Jaya shares her prize."],2,"Her chest 'felt like a balloon about to lift,' and then 'Jaya's balloon began to sink.'"],["What does Mr. Kim mean when he says 'Whatever happens, that was real'?",["The audition was not fair and must be thrown out.","Jaya earned her good performance, no matter who gets the solo.","Jaya will definitely keep the solo.","Rosa's playing was better than anyone realized."],1,"He tells her, 'you played well on Wednesday,' affirming her effort was genuine regardless of the outcome."]]},{"id":"r7_2","lv":7,"e":"🛰️","t":"The Long Voice of Voyager","p":["In 1977, NASA launched two small spacecraft named Voyager 1 and Voyager 2. Their mission was ambitious but temporary: fly past Jupiter and Saturn, snap pictures, collect data, and send it home. Engineers hoped the machines might last about five years. Nearly half a century later, both are still talking to Earth.","The Voyagers took advantage of a rare lineup of the outer planets that happens roughly once every 175 years. By swinging close to one planet, a spacecraft could borrow some of its gravity and be flung toward the next, like a skateboarder grabbing a passing railing. Voyager 2 used this trick to become the only spacecraft ever to visit Uranus and Neptune.","After their planetary tours, the probes kept going. In 2012, Voyager 1 crossed the heliopause, the boundary where the Sun's stream of charged particles gives way to the thin gas between the stars. It became the first human-made object to reach interstellar space. Voyager 2 followed in 2018, becoming the second.","Communicating across that distance is an engineering marvel. Voyager 1's radio transmitter uses only about as much power as a refrigerator light bulb. By the time its signal reaches Earth, more than twenty-two hours after it was sent, it is unimaginably faint. To catch it, NASA relies on the Deep Space Network, a set of giant dish antennas in California, Spain, and Australia. Because the stations are spread around the globe, at least one can always point toward the spacecraft as Earth turns.","Keeping the Voyagers alive has become a kind of careful rationing. Each probe runs on a nuclear power source that produces a little less electricity every year. To stretch their power, engineers have switched off cameras, heaters, and several science instruments one by one. In 2023, a single failed computer chip caused Voyager 1 to send back gibberish. Working with decades-old manuals and a wait of nearly two days to send each command and hear back, the team moved the affected code to other parts of the memory. By 2024, the spacecraft was once again sending useful data.","Someday the Voyagers will fall silent. Even then, they will keep drifting among the stars, each carrying a golden record of sounds and images from Earth, a message in a bottle tossed into the widest ocean there is."],"q":[["Which statement best expresses the central idea of the article?",["Voyager 2 is more important than Voyager 1.","Space travel is too expensive to continue.","The Voyager probes have far outlasted their planned mission thanks to clever design and engineering.","The Deep Space Network was built only for the Voyager missions."],2,"Planned for 'about five years,' the probes are 'still talking to Earth' nearly fifty years later."],["Why does the author compare a gravity assist to a skateboarder grabbing a railing?",["To help readers picture how a spacecraft uses a planet's pull to gain speed and change direction.","To suggest that the Voyagers are small and light.","To show that spacecraft sometimes crash into planets.","To explain why the planets line up every 175 years."],0,"The spacecraft could 'borrow some of its gravity and be flung toward the next.'"],["Why are the Deep Space Network antennas located on different continents?",["Each country built its own antenna for its own spacecraft.","The signals are too strong for a single dish.","Different antennas are needed for Jupiter and Neptune.","As Earth rotates, at least one station can always face the spacecraft."],3,"The article says 'at least one can always point toward the spacecraft as Earth turns.'"],["As used in the article, what does 'rationing' mean?",["Repairing broken parts with spare pieces.","Carefully limiting the use of something that is running out.","Sending messages at a faster speed.","Dividing the probes' data between countries."],1,"Engineers switch things off 'one by one' because the power source 'produces a little less electricity every year.'"],["What does the 2023 computer problem mainly show?",["Voyager 1 has already stopped working.","The Voyagers were poorly built.","Engineers can still solve problems on the probes despite huge distances and old technology.","The golden record interfered with the computer."],2,"Despite 'decades-old manuals' and a nearly two-day delay, 'by 2024, the spacecraft was once again sending useful data.'"]]},{"id":"r7_3","lv":7,"e":"🚀","t":"Katherine Johnson Checks the Numbers","p":["In February 1962, astronaut John Glenn was preparing to become the first American to orbit Earth. NASA had begun using electronic computers to calculate the path of his capsule, but Glenn was uneasy about trusting his life to a machine. Before the flight, he made a request: have Katherine Johnson check the numbers by hand. If she agreed with the computer, he said, he was ready to go.","Johnson had been preparing for moments like that her whole life. Born in 1918 in White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia, she was fascinated by counting and patterns from an early age. She raced through school, finishing high school at fourteen and graduating from college at eighteen with degrees in mathematics and French. One of her professors saw her talent and created advanced courses just for her.","In 1953, Johnson went to work at the Langley laboratory in Virginia for NACA, the agency that later became NASA. She joined a group of Black women mathematicians whose job title was, remarkably, 'computer.' They performed long, precise calculations for engineers. At that time, Virginia was segregated, and the Black computers worked in a separate office from their white colleagues.","Johnson did not stay in the background for long. She asked questions constantly and requested to attend briefings where decisions were made, even though women had not usually been invited. Over time, her curiosity and accuracy earned her a place on the team studying spaceflight. She calculated the trajectory for Alan Shepard's 1961 flight, the first time an American traveled into space.","For Glenn's orbit, Johnson spent days working through the same equations the computer had solved, digit by digit. Her results matched. Glenn flew, circled Earth three times, and returned safely. Later, Johnson helped with calculations for the Apollo 11 mission that landed astronauts on the Moon, and she continued working at NASA until 1986.","For many years, few people outside NASA knew her name. That began to change late in her life. In 2015, President Barack Obama awarded her the Presidential Medal of Freedom, one of the highest honors an American civilian can receive. Johnson died in 2020 at the age of 101, remembered not only as a brilliant mathematician but as a woman who kept walking into rooms where she had not been expected."],"q":[["Why does the author begin with John Glenn's request?",["To show how much trust NASA's astronauts placed in Johnson's accuracy.","To argue that electronic computers should never be used.","To explain how orbiting Earth works.","To prove that Glenn was afraid of flying."],0,"Glenn said that if Johnson 'agreed with the computer, he was ready to go.'"],["What does the word 'computer' mean in the third paragraph?",["An electronic machine that stores data.","A person whose job was to perform mathematical calculations.","A room where engineers held meetings.","A type of spacecraft instrument."],1,"The women 'performed long, precise calculations for engineers.'"],["How is this biography mainly organized?",["By comparing Johnson with other mathematicians.","As a list of problems and their solutions.","It opens with a key moment, then follows her life in time order.","By describing NASA's missions from newest to oldest."],2,"It starts in 1962, returns to her birth in 1918, and moves forward to 2015 and 2020."],["Which detail best supports the idea that Johnson pushed past the limits placed on her?",["She graduated with degrees in mathematics and French.","She was born in West Virginia.","She worked at NASA until 1986.","She asked to attend briefings where women had not usually been invited."],3,"She 'requested to attend briefings where decisions were made, even though women had not usually been invited.'"],["What does the final sentence suggest about how the author views Johnson?",["She was admired for both her talent and her courage in unwelcoming places.","She was mostly lucky to be in the right place at the right time.","She preferred to work quietly and avoid attention.","She was more interested in French than in mathematics."],0,"She is remembered 'as a woman who kept walking into rooms where she had not been expected.'"]]},{"id":"r7_4","lv":7,"e":"🌱","t":"The Midnight Nibbler","p":["Someone was destroying the Garden Club's lettuce. For three mornings in a row, Lena had unlocked the school greenhouse to find ragged holes chewed through the tender leaves, as though a tiny lawnmower had been let loose overnight. With the county fair only two weeks away, the club's prize-winning plan was collapsing.","'It's Grant,' declared Omar, slapping his gloves onto the potting bench. 'He's been furious ever since we beat his robotics team for the science grant. I saw him hanging around out here on Tuesday.' Lena wasn't convinced. Grant was annoying, but he was also the kind of person who got nervous returning a library book late. Besides, the greenhouse was locked every night, and only the club advisor and Lena had keys.","She decided to look at the evidence instead of the suspects. First, she noticed that the damage wasn't spread evenly. The lettuce trays sitting on the floor were shredded, while identical trays on the tall metal tables were almost untouched. Second, the worst damage appeared after Monday and Wednesday nights. Those were the evenings when the club watered heavily, leaving the floor puddled and the air thick and damp.","Then, crouching near the door, Lena spotted something that glinted in the morning light: thin, silvery trails looping across the concrete and up the sides of the floor trays. The door itself didn't quite reach the ground. Beneath it was a gap about as wide as her finger.","'Omar,' she called. 'Come look at this. I don't think our thief has any fingerprints.' Omar squinted at the shiny ribbons, then at the gap. His shoulders dropped. 'Slugs?' he groaned. 'I almost reported Grant to the principal because of slugs?' 'Slugs come out at night, they love moisture, and there isn't a single trail on the table legs,' Lena said. 'It all fits.'","That afternoon, the club moved every tray onto the tables, nailed a rubber strip along the bottom of the door, and switched to watering in the mornings so the floor could dry before dark. The next day, the lettuce was whole.","Omar spent a few minutes that evening writing a note. It wasn't exactly an apology to Grant, since Grant had never known he was a suspect, but it was an invitation to come see the greenhouse. Lena thought that was a pretty good start."],"q":[["Why did Omar first suspect Grant?",["Grant had a key to the greenhouse.","Lena saw Grant eating lettuce.","Grant left fingerprints on the trays.","Grant had a motive and had been seen near the greenhouse."],3,"Omar says Grant was 'furious' about the grant and 'I saw him hanging around out here on Tuesday.'"],["Which clue best supports the idea that the culprit was not a person?",["The fair was only two weeks away.","The club advisor had a key.","Silvery trails led across the floor from a gap under the door.","Grant got nervous about late library books."],2,"Lena finds 'thin, silvery trails' and a gap under the door, and says the thief has no fingerprints."],["Why was the damage worse after Monday and Wednesday nights?",["Those were the nights Grant stayed late at school.","Heavy watering left the greenhouse damp, which attracted the slugs.","The greenhouse door was left unlocked on those nights.","The lettuce grew faster on those days."],1,"Those evenings the floor was 'puddled and the air thick and damp,' and slugs 'love moisture.'"],["What does Lena's approach to the mystery reveal about her?",["She relies on evidence rather than jumping to conclusions.","She is quick to blame people she dislikes.","She is more interested in the fair than in the truth.","She prefers to let adults solve problems."],0,"She 'decided to look at the evidence instead of the suspects.'"],["What lesson does the ending of the story suggest?",["Science competitions create lifelong enemies.","Gardens should always be kept outdoors.","Slugs are the most dangerous garden pest.","Wrongly suspecting someone can be a reason to reach out to them."],3,"Omar writes Grant a note that is 'an invitation to come see the greenhouse.'"]]},{"id":"r7_5","lv":7,"e":"⏰","t":"Let Us Sleep: Why School Should Start Later","p":["Every weekday morning, millions of students stumble to the bus stop in the dark, backpacks sagging, eyes half closed. Adults often joke that teenagers are lazy. But science tells a different story. Most young teens are not choosing to be tired. They are fighting their own biology, and early school start times are making the fight unfair. Middle and high schools should begin no earlier than 8:30 a.m.","First, consider how the body clock changes during adolescence. As children enter their teenage years, the brain begins releasing melatonin, the hormone that signals sleepiness, later in the evening than it did before. As a result, many teens simply cannot fall asleep until around eleven o'clock, no matter how early they go to bed. If the first bell rings at 7:30, a student who must wake at six gets about seven hours of sleep at most. Sleep experts recommend eight to ten hours for teenagers.","Second, sleep is not a luxury. It is when the brain files away what it learned during the day, much like a librarian shelving returned books overnight. Students who are short on sleep have more trouble paying attention, remembering information, and controlling their moods. They are also more likely to get sick and to be involved in car accidents once they begin driving. In 2014, the American Academy of Pediatrics, a major group of children's doctors, recommended that middle and high schools start at 8:30 or later for exactly these reasons.","Critics raise fair concerns. Changing bus schedules costs money, and later dismissal could squeeze after-school sports, jobs, and family routines. These are real challenges, but they are problems of planning, not reasons to ignore students' health. Some districts that shifted their start times reorganized bus routes so younger children, whose body clocks run earlier, begin first. Many reported that students got more sleep and that attendance improved.","We would never ask athletes to compete while running on empty and then blame them for losing. Yet that is essentially what early start times ask of students every single day. Schools exist to help young people learn and grow. If we truly believe that, we should stop sounding the alarm so early and let students arrive awake, alert, and ready to think.","Contact your school board and ask a simple question: what time does our day begin, and why?"],"q":[["What is the author's main claim?",["Teenagers should go to bed earlier.","Middle and high schools should start at 8:30 a.m. or later.","After-school sports should be canceled.","Buses are too expensive for school districts."],1,"The first paragraph states, 'Middle and high schools should begin no earlier than 8:30 a.m.'"],["Which piece of evidence best supports the claim that teens cannot just go to bed earlier?",["Their brains release melatonin later in the evening during adolescence.","Changing bus schedules costs money.","Some districts reported better attendance.","Adults often joke that teenagers are lazy."],0,"Because melatonin comes later, 'many teens simply cannot fall asleep until around eleven o'clock.'"],["Why does the author include the section about critics?",["To admit that the author's argument is probably wrong.","To show that doctors disagree about sleep.","To prove that sports are more important than sleep.","To acknowledge objections and then respond to them."],3,"The author calls the concerns 'real challenges' but says they are 'problems of planning.'"],["What is the purpose of comparing the brain to a librarian shelving books?",["To suggest students should spend more time in libraries.","To show that sleep is boring but necessary.","To explain that the brain organizes and stores learning during sleep.","To argue that reading before bed causes sleepiness."],2,"Sleep 'is when the brain files away what it learned during the day.'"],["How does the author mainly try to persuade readers in the final paragraphs?",["By listing the exact cost of new bus routes.","By using a comparison to athletes and a direct call to action.","By telling a personal story about being late to school.","By quoting famous people who oppose early schools."],1,"The author compares students to athletes 'running on empty' and urges readers to 'contact your school board.'"]]},{"id":"r7_6","lv":7,"e":"👑","t":"The Golden Touch","p":["Long ago, in the land of Phrygia, there ruled a king named Midas who loved gold more than sunlight. He owned rose gardens that were the envy of every kingdom, yet he would gladly have traded every blossom for a single coin.","One morning, his servants found an old man asleep among the roses, snoring beneath a cloud of wine-scented breath. This was Silenus, the aged teacher and companion of Dionysus, god of wine and celebration. Instead of punishing the trespasser, Midas treated him as an honored guest, feasting with him for ten days before returning him safely to the god.","Dionysus was delighted. 'Ask for anything,' he told the king, 'and it will be yours.' Midas did not hesitate. 'Let everything I touch turn to gold.' The god's smile faded a little, but a promise is a promise. 'So be it,' he said.","At first, Midas's new power seemed like a dream spun from sunshine. He snapped a twig from an oak, and it glittered in his hand. He lifted a stone from the path, and it gleamed like a small sun. He ran through his palace touching doorframes and pillars, laughing as they blazed yellow. Surely, he thought, no king in history had ever been so fortunate.","Then he sat down to dinner. When he tore a piece of bread, it hardened into a golden crust that cracked his teeth. When he raised a cup of wine, the liquid became molten metal on his lips. Surrounded by a mountain of treasure, the richest man in the world was starving.","Terror crept over Midas like a cold tide. Raising his gleaming arms to the sky, he begged Dionysus to take the gift back. 'I was a fool,' he cried. 'Save me from what I wished for.' The god, seeing that the king had learned his lesson, took pity on him. 'Go to the river Pactolus,' Dionysus told him. 'Follow it to its source and plunge your head and body into the water. Wash away your greed along with your gift.'","Midas obeyed. As he stepped into the stream, the golden power flowed out of him and into the riverbed. From then on, the ancient Greeks said, the sands of the Pactolus sparkled with flecks of gold. As for Midas, he came to hate wealth and splendor, and he spent the rest of his days wandering the quiet fields and forests."],"q":[["What is the main theme of this myth?",["Kindness to strangers is always rewarded with wealth.","Gods cannot be trusted to keep their promises.","Greed can blind people to what truly matters.","Rivers were created by the gods."],2,"Midas wishes for gold but ends up 'starving' among 'a mountain of treasure' and later 'came to hate wealth.'"],["Why did Dionysus offer Midas a wish?",["Midas had built a temple for Dionysus.","Midas defeated an enemy of the god.","Midas grew the finest roses in the world.","Midas had treated the god's companion Silenus kindly."],3,"Midas treated Silenus 'as an honored guest' before 'returning him safely to the god,' and 'Dionysus was delighted.'"],["What does the detail that 'the god's smile faded a little' suggest?",["Dionysus foresaw that the wish would bring trouble.","Dionysus was angry that Midas had asked for so little.","Dionysus did not have the power to grant the wish.","Dionysus wanted the gold for himself."],0,"His reaction hints at the disaster that follows once Midas tries to eat."],["Which phrase is an example of figurative language that shows Midas's growing fear?",["'he snapped a twig from an oak'","'Terror crept over Midas like a cold tide'","'he sat down to dinner'","'follow it to its source'"],1,"The simile compares his fear to a tide slowly rising over him."],["According to the myth, what explains the gold in the river Pactolus?",["Midas washed his golden power away into the river's sands.","Dionysus filled the river with coins as a reward.","Midas threw his treasure into the river in anger.","Silenus dropped gold there while sleeping."],0,"As Midas entered the stream, 'the golden power flowed out of him and into the riverbed.'"]]},{"id":"r8_1","lv":8,"e":"📓","t":"The Notebook on the Bus","p":["Priya found the notebook wedged between the seat and the window of the number nine bus, its green cover soft from handling. She knew it before she opened it. Every student in Ms. Okafor's science class knew that notebook, because Marcus Hale carried it everywhere like a second heartbeat. Inside, in cramped handwriting, were three months of measurements for his project on how road salt affects the growth of bean seedlings.","The regional science fair was on Saturday. Only one student from their school would advance to the state competition, and everyone said it would be either Priya or Marcus. Priya had spent her own three months testing water filters made from sand, charcoal and gravel, and she was proud of her work. Still, she had watched Marcus explain his data to the class, and she had felt something inside her shrink. He spoke about his seedlings the way some people speak about their friends.","Now his friends were in her backpack.","She did not take the notebook out again until she was home, sitting on her bed with the door closed. She turned the pages slowly. There were no photocopies here, no backup charts. Marcus had mentioned once that his laptop had crashed in October and that he trusted paper more than machines. Without this notebook, he would stand in front of the judges with a poster and nothing to prove his numbers were real.","'Nobody would know,' said a quiet voice in her head. It did not sound evil. It sounded reasonable, almost kind, the way a shortcut looks kind at the end of a long day. She would not be stealing anything. She would simply be slow to return something. The fair was in three days. Things got lost on buses all the time.","Priya's grandmother, who had lived with the family since Priya was six, knocked and came in with a cup of tea. She noticed the notebook at once, because she noticed everything. Priya explained, and then, to her own surprise, she explained the rest of it too: the fair, the state competition, the voice.","Her grandmother sipped her tea and said nothing for a while. Finally she said, 'When I was young, I won a spelling contest because the girl who should have won was sick that day. I kept the ribbon in a drawer for forty years. I never once took it out to look at it.'","The next morning Priya got to school early and waited by Marcus's locker. When he saw the green cover in her hand, his face went through several weathers at once: confusion, relief, and then something close to suspicion, as though he could not quite believe she was giving it back. 'I found it on the bus,' she said simply. He thanked her twice. On Saturday, Marcus won first place, and Priya won second. Walking out of the gym, she realized that her disappointment was real but light, like a backpack with nothing hidden inside it."],"q":[["Which statement best expresses the theme of the story?",["Hard work always leads to winning first place.","Science fairs create unhealthy rivalries between friends.","A victory gained dishonestly would carry a burden that losing honestly does not.","Grandparents usually know more than parents about school."],2,"The grandmother's unlooked-at ribbon and Priya's 'light' disappointment show that honesty spares her a hidden weight."],["Why does the author include the detail that Marcus's laptop crashed in October?",["To show that the notebook was his only record, which raises the stakes of Priya's choice","To suggest that Marcus is careless with his belongings","To explain why Marcus sits in the back of the bus","To prove that Priya had read his project secretly"],0,"The text says 'There were no photocopies here, no backup charts,' so losing the notebook would ruin his project."],["What does the grandmother's story about the spelling ribbon suggest?",["She regretted entering contests at all.","She was proud of her spelling ability for forty years.","She thinks Priya should keep the notebook.","The prize she did not truly earn brought her no joy."],3,"She 'never once took it out to look at it,' showing the undeserved win meant nothing to her."],["What does the phrase 'his face went through several weathers at once' mean?",["Marcus looked sick after riding the cold bus.","Marcus showed a quick series of different emotions.","Marcus was angry that Priya had read his notebook.","Marcus pretended not to recognize the notebook."],1,"The next words list the 'weathers': 'confusion, relief, and then something close to suspicion.'"],["Why does the author describe the inner voice as sounding 'reasonable, almost kind'?",["To show that Priya is a cruel person","To show that her grandmother is speaking to her","To show how temptation can disguise itself as sensible thinking","To show that keeping the notebook would actually help Marcus"],2,"The voice compares itself to 'a shortcut' and argues she would 'simply be slow,' making a wrong choice sound harmless."]]},{"id":"r8_2","lv":8,"e":"🌉","t":"How Bridges Hold Us Up","p":["Every time a car rolls across a bridge, an invisible struggle takes place beneath its tires. Gravity pulls the car, the bridge and everything else toward the earth, and the bridge must push back. Engineers describe this contest using two fundamental forces: compression and tension. Understanding how a bridge manages these forces explains why bridges come in such different shapes, from humble stone arches to soaring suspension spans.","Compression is a squeezing force. When you press the ends of a sponge together, you compress it. Tension is the opposite, a stretching or pulling force, like the force in a rope during tug-of-war. Materials respond to these forces differently. Stone and concrete resist compression extremely well but crack easily when stretched. Steel cables, by contrast, are superb in tension but would simply buckle if you tried to push on them. A good bridge design therefore places each material where its strength is useful and its weakness is protected.","The simplest bridge is a beam, such as a plank laid across a stream. When you stand in the middle, the plank bends slightly. Its top surface is squeezed together while its bottom surface is stretched apart. This is why a long beam made of a brittle material eventually snaps from the bottom. Engineers extend the reach of beams by adding trusses, networks of triangles that spread the load. A triangle is valuable because it cannot change shape unless one of its sides changes length, so it keeps its form under heavy weight.","The arch is one of humanity's oldest solutions. Ancient Roman builders constructed arched bridges and aqueducts, some of which still stand after two thousand years. In an arch, the weight of the load is redirected outward and downward along the curve, so every stone is pressed tightly against its neighbors. The entire structure is in compression, which suits stone perfectly. However, an arch pushes sideways against the ground at each end, so it needs solid supports, called abutments, to keep it from spreading apart.","Suspension bridges reverse the strategy. Their roadway hangs from vertical cables attached to enormous main cables, which drape over tall towers and are anchored firmly into the ground at each end. The load travels up through the hangers and into the main cables, which are in tension. The towers, meanwhile, are pressed downward in compression. Because steel cable is so strong for its weight, suspension bridges can cross distances that no stone arch could span.","Forces are not the only challenge. Wind, temperature and even rhythm can threaten a bridge. In 1940, the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington State twisted violently in a steady wind and collapsed only months after it opened; no people were killed, but the disaster taught engineers to study how air flows around a structure. Modern bridges include expansion joints that let the deck lengthen on hot days and shrink on cold ones.","A bridge, in the end, is an argument with gravity that must be won every second of every day. The winning strategy is never brute strength alone, but the careful matching of shape and material to the forces they must carry."],"q":[["What is the central idea of the passage?",["Stone arches are stronger than all modern bridges.","Bridges succeed by matching shapes and materials to the forces of compression and tension.","The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapse changed how cars are built.","Engineers prefer steel because it resists every kind of force."],1,"The conclusion states the winning strategy is 'the careful matching of shape and material to the forces they must carry.'"],["Based on the passage, why would a stone arch be a poor choice for a very long span?",["Stone cannot handle compression.","Arches need no abutments, so they slide away.","Wind blows more strongly over stone.","Stone is heavy and weak in tension, while long spans are better served by strong, light steel cable."],3,"The text says steel cable is 'so strong for its weight' that suspension bridges 'can cross distances that no stone arch could span.'"],["In paragraph 4, what does the word 'abutments' refer to?",["Solid supports at each end that keep an arch from spreading","The curved stones at the top of an arch","Cables that hold up a roadway","Joints that let a deck expand"],0,"The passage defines them as 'solid supports, called abutments, to keep it from spreading apart.'"],["How does paragraph 3 develop the author's explanation?",["It argues that beams should never be used.","It describes the history of Roman aqueducts.","It uses a plank to show compression and tension acting in one beam and explains how triangles add strength.","It compares the costs of different bridge types."],2,"It explains the plank's 'top surface is squeezed' while the bottom 'is stretched,' then describes trusses of triangles."],["Why does the author mention the Tacoma Narrows Bridge?",["To prove that suspension bridges are unsafe","To show that forces like wind also matter and that failures taught engineers new lessons","To criticize engineers in Washington State","To explain how expansion joints were invented"],1,"The paragraph opens 'Forces are not the only challenge' and says the collapse 'taught engineers to study how air flows.'"]]},{"id":"r8_3","lv":8,"e":"📡","t":"A Wire Across the Ocean","p":["In the 1850s, a message sent from London to New York traveled no faster than the ship that carried it, which meant a journey of roughly ten days or more. News of wars, prices and elections was always stale by the time it crossed the Atlantic. The electric telegraph had already transformed communication on land, sending coded pulses along wires in minutes. The question that obsessed a determined American businessman named Cyrus Field was whether a wire could be laid across the bottom of the ocean itself.","Many experts considered the idea absurd. The cable would have to stretch roughly two thousand miles, sink more than two miles deep in places, and survive crushing pressure and cold. Field was not a scientist, but he was a skilled organizer and fundraiser. He gathered investors in both Britain and the United States, and the two governments lent ships to the project.","The early attempts were humbling. In 1857, the cable snapped and vanished into the sea after several hundred miles had been laid. In 1858, the expedition tried a new plan: two ships met in the middle of the ocean, spliced their cables together, and sailed in opposite directions. The cable broke again, more than once. Newspapers began to mock the venture as a costly fantasy.","Then, in August 1858, the cable was finally connected from Ireland to Newfoundland. Celebrations erupted. Queen Victoria sent a message of congratulations to President James Buchanan, and cities in America held parades and fireworks. Yet the triumph was fragile. The signal was so weak that the Queen's short message took many hours to transmit. An electrician, hoping to strengthen the signal, applied very high voltage, and within about three weeks the cable fell silent forever. Some people even claimed the whole thing had been a hoax.","Field refused to surrender, though the Civil War interrupted his efforts for years. When the project resumed, it had an extraordinary new tool: the Great Eastern, an enormous steamship that was far larger than any other vessel of its time. It could carry the entire length of cable in its hold, removing the need for a risky mid-ocean splice. Engineers also designed a thicker, better-insulated cable, and scientists such as William Thomson, later known as Lord Kelvin, had developed more sensitive instruments for detecting faint signals.","An 1865 attempt failed when the cable broke and was lost. But in July 1866, the Great Eastern successfully landed a working cable in Newfoundland. Remarkably, the crew then returned to the ocean, hooked the lost 1865 cable from the seafloor, spliced it, and completed a second line. Messages that once took more than a week now took minutes.","The transatlantic cable did not merely speed up the news. It knitted distant economies together, allowing merchants and governments to react to events almost as they happened. It also demonstrated something about progress itself: great achievements often grow out of a stubborn pile of failures, each one quietly teaching the lesson that the next attempt requires."],"q":[["What was the main reason the 1858 cable was considered a 'fragile' triumph?",["The Queen refused to use it.","It connected the wrong cities.","The ships that laid it sank soon afterward.","Its signal was weak, and it stopped working within weeks."],3,"The text says the signal 'was so weak' and 'within about three weeks the cable fell silent forever.'"],["Which inference is best supported by the description of Cyrus Field?",["He personally invented the telegraph.","He was trained as an electrical scientist.","His persistence and organizing skill mattered as much as technical knowledge.","He lost interest after the 1858 failure."],2,"Field 'was not a scientist, but he was a skilled organizer' and 'refused to surrender.'"],["Why was the Great Eastern especially important to the 1866 success?",["It was the fastest ship on the Atlantic.","It could carry all the cable at once, avoiding a risky mid-ocean splice.","It was owned by Queen Victoria.","It carried sensitive instruments that Field invented."],1,"The passage says it 'could carry the entire length of cable in its hold, removing the need for a risky mid-ocean splice.'"],["What is the most likely purpose of paragraph 1?",["To show how slow transatlantic news was, so readers understand why the cable mattered","To explain how the telegraph code worked","To argue that ships were more reliable than wires","To describe Cyrus Field's childhood"],0,"It explains messages traveled 'no faster than the ship' and news 'was always stale,' setting up the problem."],["What theme does the final paragraph express?",["Businessmen should avoid risky projects.","Newspapers are usually right to mock new ideas.","Technology always succeeds on the first try when enough money is spent.","Repeated failures can teach the lessons that make later success possible."],3,"It says 'great achievements often grow out of a stubborn pile of failures, each one quietly teaching the lesson.'"]]},{"id":"r8_4","lv":8,"e":"⚙️","t":"Ada Lovelace and the Thinking Engine","p":["In the 1840s, long before anyone owned a computer, a young English mathematician imagined what one might someday do. Her name was Ada Lovelace, and the notes she wrote about a machine that was never finished are now celebrated as one of the earliest visions of modern computing.","Ada was born Augusta Ada Byron in London in 1815. Her father was Lord Byron, one of the most famous and scandalous poets in Europe. Her parents separated when she was only weeks old, and Byron soon left England forever; he died in Greece in 1824, when Ada was eight, and she never truly knew him. Her mother, Annabella Milbanke, was a serious woman with a strong interest in mathematics. Partly to steer her daughter away from what she saw as her father's wild, poetic temperament, she made sure Ada received rigorous lessons in mathematics and science, an unusual education for a girl of that era.","The plan did not entirely work, and that turned out to be fortunate. Ada never stopped loving imagination; instead, she fused it with logic. She later described her own approach as 'poetical science,' a way of thinking that used creativity to see where careful reasoning might lead.","In 1833, at seventeen, Ada attended a gathering where she met Charles Babbage, a mathematician and inventor. Babbage showed guests a working portion of his Difference Engine, a mechanical calculator built of brass gears. Many visitors saw only a clever gadget. Ada, according to accounts of the evening, grasped how it worked and why it mattered. The two began a friendship and correspondence that lasted for years. In 1835 she married William King, who later became the Earl of Lovelace, making her the Countess of Lovelace.","Babbage soon designed an even more ambitious machine, the Analytical Engine. Unlike a calculator that performed one fixed task, it was meant to follow instructions supplied on punched cards, an idea borrowed from looms that wove patterns into cloth. In 1842, an Italian engineer, Luigi Menabrea, published an article in French describing the machine. Ada translated it into English and added her own commentary. Her notes grew to roughly three times the length of the original article.","Those notes, published in 1843, contained two remarkable contributions. First, she laid out a detailed, step-by-step method for the engine to calculate a series of values called Bernoulli numbers. Many historians regard this as one of the first published computer programs. Second, and perhaps more visionary, she suggested that such a machine need not be limited to numbers. If symbols such as musical notes could be represented in its system, she wrote, the engine might one day compose elaborate pieces of music.","The Analytical Engine was never built, largely because of its cost and the limits of the era's engineering. Ada died in 1852 at only thirty-six. For nearly a century her notes attracted little attention. When electronic computers finally arrived in the twentieth century, however, researchers rediscovered her work and recognized how far ahead she had seen. In 1980, the United States Department of Defense even named a programming language 'Ada' in her honor. Her life suggests that the boldest science may begin not with a finished machine, but with the courage to imagine one."],"q":[["Why did Ada's mother insist on lessons in mathematics and science?",["She hoped to steer Ada away from her father's wild, poetic temperament.","She wanted Ada to work for Charles Babbage.","The law required girls to study mathematics.","Ada had asked to become an engineer."],0,"The text says it was 'partly to steer her daughter away from what she saw as her father's wild, poetic temperament.'"],["What does the phrase 'The plan did not entirely work, and that turned out to be fortunate' suggest?",["Ada failed her mathematics lessons.","Ada's imagination, combined with logic, helped her see possibilities others missed.","Ada gave up science to write poetry.","Ada's mother regretted teaching her."],1,"Ada 'never stopped loving imagination; instead, she fused it with logic,' which she called 'poetical science.'"],["Which detail best shows Ada's vision went beyond calculation?",["She married William King in 1835.","She translated an article from French.","She suggested the engine might compose music if notes were represented as symbols.","She met Babbage at a gathering."],2,"She proposed the machine 'need not be limited to numbers' and 'might one day compose elaborate pieces of music.'"],["How does paragraph 5 contribute to the passage?",["It explains why the Analytical Engine was never built.","It describes Ada's childhood education.","It lists the awards Ada received during her life.","It explains the Analytical Engine and how Ada came to write her notes about it."],3,"It describes the punched-card machine, Menabrea's article, and Ada's translation with notes 'three times the length.'"],["What is the central idea of the final paragraph?",["Ada's imaginative work was recognized as groundbreaking once computers existed.","The Analytical Engine was too expensive to matter.","Ada's notes were famous during her lifetime.","Programming languages are usually named after engineers."],0,"Her notes were ignored 'for nearly a century' until researchers 'recognized how far ahead she had seen.'"]]},{"id":"r8_5","lv":8,"e":"🚲","t":"Make Room for Bikes","p":["Picture a main street at eight in the morning. Cars idle bumper to bumper, each carrying a single tired driver, while a lone cyclist squeezes along the curb, glancing nervously over her shoulder. Most people in that traffic jam could not imagine biking to work, and for good reason: the street was not built for them to do it safely. Cities should build connected networks of protected bike lanes, because doing so makes streets safer, healthier and more efficient for everyone.","The first reason is safety. A painted stripe offers little comfort when a delivery truck roars past at forty miles per hour. Protected bike lanes, by contrast, separate riders from traffic with curbs, posts or rows of parked cars. Many people who say they would like to ride but feel too afraid are responding sensibly to real danger. When the danger is reduced, a much wider range of people, including teenagers, parents and older adults, can choose to ride.","The second reason is health, both personal and public. Regular cycling builds fitness without requiring a gym membership or extra time, because the exercise happens during a trip a person needed to make anyway. Every trip taken by bicycle instead of by car also means less exhaust in the air that everyone breathes.","The third reason is efficiency. A lane of road can move far more people on bicycles than in cars, because a bicycle takes up only a fraction of the space. Even drivers benefit when some of their neighbors switch to bikes, since each cyclist is one fewer car competing for the same pavement and the same parking spaces. Building bike lanes also typically costs much less than widening roads, which often fill with new traffic soon after they open.","Opponents raise several objections. Shop owners worry that removing parking spaces to make room for bike lanes will drive customers away. Others point out that cycling is unpleasant in heavy rain, snow or on steep hills, so lanes might sit empty for much of the year. Some drivers simply resent giving up space they have always used.","These concerns are understandable, but they do not defeat the case. Business owners often overestimate how many of their customers arrive by car, and cyclists tend to stop at local shops more frequently, even if they spend a little less per visit. Weather is a real limitation, yet many riders adapt with proper clothing, and lanes need not be full every day to be worth building; no one argues that roads should be removed because traffic is light on holidays. As for hills, electric bicycles have made steep routes manageable for ordinary riders.","A city's streets are its largest public space, and how that space is divided reflects what a community values. For decades, most American streets have been designed almost entirely around the car. Adding protected bike lanes does not declare war on drivers. It simply offers people a genuine choice, and a city that gives its residents more safe choices is a city that works better for all of them."],"q":[["Why does the author open with a description of a traffic jam and a nervous cyclist?",["To show that cyclists cause traffic jams","To create a vivid picture of the problem the essay will address","To argue that cars should be banned","To describe the author's own commute"],1,"The scene shows crowded cars and an unsafe cyclist, then states 'the street was not built for them to do it safely.'"],["According to the author, why do many people avoid biking even if they would like to ride?",["They do not own bicycles.","They prefer to exercise at a gym.","They live too far from work.","They reasonably fear riding next to fast traffic."],3,"Paragraph 2 says people 'who say they would like to ride but feel too afraid are responding sensibly to real danger.'"],["How does the author respond to the concern that bad weather will leave lanes empty?",["By admitting that bike lanes should only be built in warm cities","By comparing it to roads, which are not removed just because traffic is light on some days","By claiming that it never rains in most cities","By suggesting lanes be closed during winter"],1,"The author writes that 'no one argues that roads should be removed because traffic is light on holidays.'"],["Which statement best describes the purpose of paragraph 5?",["It presents opposing arguments that the author then answers.","It gives the author's strongest reason for bike lanes.","It concludes the essay with a call to action.","It provides statistics about bicycle sales."],0,"It begins 'Opponents raise several objections' and lists them before paragraph 6 responds."],["What does the author suggest by saying bike lanes do 'not declare war on drivers'?",["Drivers should be required to bike once a week.","Drivers have lost the argument about city streets.","Bike lanes add options for residents rather than attacking those who drive.","Cyclists and drivers are usually in conflict."],2,"The next sentence says bike lanes 'simply offer people a genuine choice.'"]]},{"id":"r8_6","lv":8,"e":"🔍","t":"The Lighthouse Key","p":["The Harbor Museum closed at five o'clock, and by five-fifteen the brass key to the old lighthouse was missing. It was not valuable in itself, only a heavy, tarnished thing the length of a hand, but the museum's director, Mrs. Delgado, had planned to display it the next morning at a ceremony honoring the last lighthouse keeper. Now its velvet case sat open and empty on her desk.","Theo, who volunteered at the museum on Thursdays, had been sorting postcards in the gift shop all afternoon. When Mrs. Delgado called the staff into her office, he came too, partly out of loyalty and partly out of a curiosity he was trying hard to hide.","There were three suspects, because only three people besides Theo and Mrs. Delgado had been in the building after four. Mr. Pruitt, the security guard, said he had been at the front entrance the entire time, which was confirmed by the visitor log he had signed at every hour. Ruth, the young curator, said she had been in the basement archive since three-thirty, cataloging photographs, and had come upstairs only when she heard her name. Dennis, a delivery driver who brought boxes of brochures, said he had dropped them at the back door around four-forty and left immediately.","Theo looked around the office carefully. The window behind the desk was closed, and rain was streaking down the glass. It had started to pour at about four-thirty; he remembered because the gift shop had suddenly filled with visitors shaking out umbrellas. He glanced at Dennis, whose jacket was dark and soaked across the shoulders. That made sense for a man who had carried boxes through the rain.","Then he looked at Ruth. Her cardigan was dry, but the cuffs of her trousers were wet, and a small puddle was forming around her shoes on the carpet. A single damp leaf clung to one heel.","'Ruth,' Theo said slowly, 'the archive is in the basement, and there are no windows or doors to the outside down there. How did your shoes get wet?'","Ruth's face flushed. For a long moment she said nothing. Then she reached into her cardigan pocket and placed the lighthouse key on the desk.","She had not meant to steal it, she explained. Her grandfather had been the assistant keeper at that lighthouse decades ago, and she had wanted to unlock the old door one last time before the key disappeared behind glass. During the downpour she had hurried across the wet garden path to the lighthouse, found that the lock had been changed years before, and rushed back, ashamed and soaked below the knees.","Mrs. Delgado was quiet for a while. Then she said, 'Tomorrow, I think the ceremony should include someone who can tell the story of your grandfather.' Theo returned to his postcards, surprised to find that solving the mystery felt less like winning and more like opening a door."],"q":[["Which clue revealed that Ruth had not stayed in the basement the whole time?",["Her cardigan pocket was bulging.","She came upstairs when she heard her name.","Her shoes and trouser cuffs were wet, though the basement had no way outside.","She had signed the visitor log every hour."],2,"Theo notes her wet cuffs and shoes and asks how they got wet when the basement has 'no windows or doors to the outside.'"],["Why does the author mention that the rain started at about four-thirty?",["It establishes a time frame that makes wet clothing meaningful evidence.","It explains why the museum closed early.","It shows that Dennis lied about his delivery.","It proves that Mr. Pruitt left the entrance."],0,"Anyone wet had to have been outside after four-thirty, which exposes Ruth's claim to be in the basement since three-thirty."],["What was Ruth's main motivation for taking the key?",["She planned to sell it for money.","She wanted to embarrass Mrs. Delgado.","She wanted to hide it from Dennis.","She wanted to open the lighthouse door her grandfather had once tended."],3,"Her grandfather 'had been the assistant keeper' and she 'wanted to unlock the old door one last time.'"],["Why is Dennis's soaked jacket NOT suspicious?",["Dennis was never in the building.","He had a sensible reason to be wet: carrying boxes through the rain.","Mr. Pruitt saw Dennis take the key.","His jacket dried before Theo noticed it."],1,"Theo thinks, 'That made sense for a man who had carried boxes through the rain.'"],["What does Mrs. Delgado's response at the end suggest about her?",["She values understanding and fairness over simple punishment.","She plans to cancel the ceremony.","She believes Theo stole the key.","She is angry that the lock was changed."],0,"Instead of punishing Ruth, she invites someone to 'tell the story of your grandfather' at the ceremony."]]},{"id":"r9_1","lv":9,"e":"🎹","t":"The Tuner's Apprentice","p":["Mr. Halvorsen had tuned pianos in the valley for forty-one years, and he still carried his tools in the cracked leather case his father had given him. The summer Ines turned fifteen, her mother arranged for her to carry that case, drive nowhere, and learn. 'He needs the help,' her mother said, in the careful voice adults use when a sentence has a second half they have decided not to say.","The second half revealed itself slowly. Mr. Halvorsen asked people to repeat themselves. He tilted his left ear toward the soundboard as though leaning over a well to hear whether it still held water. At the Pruitt farmhouse, he struck the same A four times, frowned, and then struck it a fifth time with a kind of anger Ines had never seen in him.","Still, his hands knew what his ears were losing. He taught her to listen for the beats, the faint wavering pulse that appears when two strings are almost, but not quite, in agreement. 'A piano is never in tune,' he told her, setting the tuning hammer on a pin. 'It is only in a truce. Our job is to negotiate a truce that lasts until spring.'","She loved the vocabulary of the work: unisons, temperament, the stretch of the octaves toward the top of the keyboard, where a perfectly mathematical tuning would sound flat to human ears and so had to be nudged slightly sharp. She loved that the correct answer was sometimes a pleasing lie. By July she could hear the beats before he did. By August she could hear what he could not hear at all.","The church piano on Mill Road was their last job of the season. The choir director, a brisk woman named Mrs. Okafor, had a Sunday concert planned, and she hovered near the pews while Mr. Halvorsen worked. He moved through the middle octaves with his old confidence, humming under his breath. Then he reached the high treble, set his hammer, turned, listened, and nodded. 'There,' he said. 'Clean as rainwater.'","It was not clean. To Ines, the note shimmered with a quick, sour beat, like a moth trapped behind glass. He moved to the next note and pronounced it clean as well. It was worse. Four notes, five, then eight, each one declared finished, each one quietly wrong. Mrs. Okafor, who could not have told a unison from a lamppost, smiled and thanked him.","In the truck afterward, the heat pressing through the windshield, he asked her how it had sounded. He did not look at her when he asked. He kept his hands on the steering wheel, though the engine was not yet running, and Ines understood that he had asked the question the way a person steps onto ice, testing, hoping, already half aware.","She thought about the beats, about truces and pleasing lies, about the forty-one years printed in the leather of the case at her feet. She thought about Sunday, and the sopranos reaching for a note that would not quite be there to meet them. She thought that there were two kinds of kindness and that she was only fifteen and had not been told which one this was.","'It sounded,' she began, and then the cicadas rose in the field beside the church, loud as static, and for a moment neither of them could hear anything at all. When the noise settled, Mr. Halvorsen started the engine. Ines looked at the church windows, then down at her hands, which already knew the weight of the hammer, and she finished her sentence."],"q":[["Which statement best expresses a central theme of the story?",["Honesty and kindness can pull in opposite directions, making the truth difficult to tell.","Hard work and practice will always overcome physical limitations.","Young people understand music better than older people do.","Religious communities value performance over skill."],0,"Ines reflects that 'there were two kinds of kindness' and cannot decide whether to tell Mr. Halvorsen the notes were wrong."],["What does Ines's mother most likely leave unsaid when she says, 'He needs the help'?",["That Mr. Halvorsen cannot afford to pay a real assistant.","That Ines needs a summer job to stay out of trouble.","That Mr. Halvorsen plans to retire and give Ines his business.","That Mr. Halvorsen is losing his hearing and may not be able to work alone."],3,"The 'second half revealed itself slowly' as he asks people to repeat themselves and strains to hear the soundboard."],["In context, Mr. Halvorsen's description of a tuned piano as 'only in a truce' suggests that",["tuning is a temporary compromise rather than a permanent perfection.","pianos are dangerous instruments that must be handled carefully.","he dislikes pianos and views his work as a battle.","churches and farmhouses often argue about how pianos should sound."],0,"He says a piano 'is never in tune' and that the job is to make a truce 'that lasts until spring.'"],["Why does the author compare Mr. Halvorsen's question to 'the way a person steps onto ice'?",["To show that he is angry with Ines for doubting him.","To suggest that the truck is cold despite the summer heat.","To reveal that he has decided to quit his job immediately.","To suggest he suspects the truth and fears what her answer will confirm."],3,"The simile describes him as 'testing, hoping, already half aware,' and he avoids looking at her."],["How does the ending of the story shape its meaning?",["It reveals that Mrs. Okafor secretly noticed the mistakes.","By withholding Ines's final words, it leaves readers to weigh the dilemma themselves.","It proves that Ines chose to lie so that the concert would go smoothly.","It shows that the cicadas made the church piano sound out of tune."],1,"The story says only that 'she finished her sentence,' never telling us what she said."]]},{"id":"r9_2","lv":9,"e":"🛰️","t":"Why Your Phone Needs Einstein","p":["When a phone app announces that you should turn left in three hundred feet, it is quietly performing one of the most elegant experiments in the history of physics. The Global Positioning System works only because engineers took seriously two ideas that once sounded absurd: that time moves at different rates depending on how fast you travel, and that it also depends on how strongly gravity pulls on you.","The basic method is simple enough. About thirty GPS satellites circle the Earth roughly twenty thousand kilometers overhead, each carrying extraordinarily precise atomic clocks. Every satellite continuously broadcasts a signal that essentially says, 'I am here, and this is the exact time.' Your phone receives signals from several satellites at once, notes how long each one took to arrive, and, because radio waves travel at the speed of light, converts those delays into distances. With distances to at least four satellites, the receiver can calculate its position on the planet.","The catch lies in that phrase 'the speed of light.' Light covers about thirty centimeters in a single billionth of a second. A timing error of only one microsecond, one millionth of a second, therefore translates into a position error of roughly three hundred meters. In other words, GPS is less a system of maps than a system of clocks, and it is only as good as their agreement.","Here is where Albert Einstein enters. His special theory of relativity, published in 1905, predicts that a moving clock ticks more slowly than a stationary one. The satellites travel at nearly four kilometers per second, and as a result their clocks fall behind clocks on the ground by about seven microseconds each day.","His general theory of relativity, completed a decade later, adds a second and opposite effect. Clocks deeper in a gravitational field run more slowly than clocks farther away from it. Because the satellites orbit far above the surface, where Earth's gravity is weaker, their clocks run faster than ours by about forty-five microseconds per day.","The two effects do not cancel. Subtract the seven microseconds lost to speed from the forty-five gained from altitude, and the satellite clocks run ahead by about thirty-eight microseconds every day. That sounds trivial until you remember the arithmetic above. Left uncorrected, the error would accumulate into something on the order of ten kilometers of drift per day, enough to send a delivery truck into the wrong town by dinnertime.","Engineers solved the problem before the first satellites were launched. They deliberately set the satellite clocks to tick at a slightly slower rate before sending them into orbit, so that, once aloft, relativity would speed them up to match the ground. Receivers apply further small corrections, since orbits are not perfectly circular. According to accounts of the system's early history, some engineers doubted that relativity would matter in practice, and the correction was built so that it could be switched off. Measurements from orbit confirmed that Einstein's predictions held, and the correction has remained in place ever since, a quiet tribute from practical engineers to a theory many of them had once considered little more than an abstraction.","There is a pleasing irony in all this. Relativity is often described as a theory of the remote and the enormous, of black holes and galaxies and light bending around the sun. Yet its most familiar confirmation sits in your pocket, guiding you to a coffee shop. Every time a blue dot settles on the right street corner, the universe is repeating a verdict first reached on paper more than a century ago: time is not the same for everyone, and the difference is measurable."],"q":[["What is the central idea of the passage?",["GPS satellites are expensive and difficult to maintain.","GPS accuracy depends on correcting clocks for effects predicted by relativity.","Einstein personally helped design the first navigation satellites.","Phones use maps rather than clocks to find their location."],1,"The passage says GPS 'works only because engineers took seriously' relativity and corrected the clocks."],["Why does the author explain that light travels about thirty centimeters in a billionth of a second?",["To show why tiny timing errors produce large position errors.","To prove that radio waves are slower than visible light.","To explain why satellites must orbit so far from Earth.","To argue that phones should use lasers instead of radio."],0,"The next sentence states that a one-microsecond error becomes 'roughly three hundred meters' of position error."],["Based on the passage, what would most likely happen if the satellite clocks were not adjusted?",["The satellites would slowly fall out of orbit.","Phones would stop receiving signals entirely.","Locations would grow steadily less accurate each day.","The clocks would eventually correct themselves."],2,"The author says the error 'would accumulate into something on the order of ten kilometers of drift per day.'"],["The phrase 'less a system of maps than a system of clocks' is meant to emphasize that",["GPS companies care more about time than about geography.","printed maps have become unnecessary in modern life.","satellites display the time on phones more accurately than maps.","the system's precision rests fundamentally on accurate timekeeping."],3,"The sentence concludes that GPS 'is only as good as their agreement,' referring to the clocks."],["What irony does the author point out in the final paragraph?",["Einstein never believed his own theories would be tested.","Engineers ignored relativity and succeeded anyway.","A theory associated with vast cosmic objects is confirmed by an everyday device.","Coffee shops are harder to locate than galaxies."],2,"Relativity is 'often described as a theory of the remote and the enormous,' yet 'its most familiar confirmation sits in your pocket.'"]]},{"id":"r9_3","lv":9,"e":"📜","t":"The Machine That Multiplied Words","p":["Before the middle of the fifteenth century, a book in Europe was a slow and costly thing. Each one had to be copied by hand, often by monks or professional scribes working letter by letter over months. A single mistake might be faithfully reproduced in every copy made from it, and a modest library could represent the wealth of a small estate. Knowledge existed, but it moved at the pace of a tired wrist.","Around 1450, in the German city of Mainz, a goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg brought together several existing technologies into a new system. He developed a way to cast large numbers of identical metal letters, called movable type, that could be arranged into pages, inked, and pressed onto paper, then taken apart and reused. He adapted a screw press similar to those used for pressing grapes and olives, and he experimented with an oil-based ink that would cling to metal. None of these ideas was entirely unprecedented; movable type had been developed centuries earlier in China and Korea. Gutenberg's achievement lay in combining them into a process efficient enough to change a continent.","His most famous product, a Latin Bible completed around 1455, is still admired for the beauty of its pages. Historians estimate that roughly 180 copies were printed. That number may seem small, but a scribe might have spent years producing just one. Suddenly, a single workshop could accomplish in months what whole communities of copyists could not have managed in a lifetime, and every copy would say exactly the same thing, down to the placement of each letter on the page.","The technology spread with remarkable speed. By 1500, printing presses were operating in more than two hundred European cities, and historians estimate that millions of books had been produced. The price of books fell, and printers, who were business owners seeking customers, began to publish not only religious works but also grammars, calendars, medical guides, travel accounts, and pamphlets in the everyday languages people spoke rather than in Latin alone. Reading, once the privilege of clergy and the wealthy, gradually became a skill worth acquiring for merchants, artisans, and their children.","The consequences soon became impossible to ignore. In 1517, a monk and professor named Martin Luther wrote a set of arguments challenging practices of the Catholic Church. In earlier centuries, such criticism might have circulated among a few scholars and faded. Instead, printers reproduced Luther's writings rapidly, and within a few years his ideas had reached readers across the German lands and beyond. The Protestant Reformation that followed reshaped the religious and political map of Europe. Luther himself reportedly called printing one of God's greatest gifts.","Printing also transformed science. When scholars could share identical diagrams, tables, and measurements, they could check one another's work, build on it, and correct it. An astronomer in one country could study the same star chart as a colleague hundreds of miles away. Errors, once printed, could be publicly identified and fixed in the next edition rather than silently multiplied.","Not everyone welcomed the change. Some scholars worried that cheap books would spread falsehoods as easily as truths, and authorities in several places tried to control what could be printed. Their concern was not entirely wrong: pamphlets carried rumors, propaganda, and attacks alongside serious ideas. The press amplified whatever was fed into it.","That double edge is perhaps the most enduring lesson of Gutenberg's invention. A tool that makes words cheap does not decide which words deserve attention. It hands that responsibility to readers, who must learn to weigh, question, and compare. Every new medium since, from newspapers to the internet, has returned Europe's fifteenth-century dilemma to a new generation."],"q":[["Which sentence best states the central idea of the passage?",["Gutenberg was the first person in history to invent movable type.","The printing press spread information widely, bringing major benefits along with new risks.","Martin Luther could not have succeeded without Gutenberg's personal support.","Hand-copied books were more beautiful than printed ones."],1,"The passage traces the press's effects on religion and science, then notes 'the press amplified whatever was fed into it.'"],["What does the phrase 'Knowledge existed, but it moved at the pace of a tired wrist' suggest?",["Scribes often suffered injuries from their work.","Few people in Europe were interested in learning.","Books were mostly written about physical labor.","Copying by hand made the spread of ideas slow and limited."],3,"The metaphor follows the description of books copied 'letter by letter over months.'"],["Why does the author mention that movable type had been developed earlier in China and Korea?",["To clarify that Gutenberg's contribution was combining technologies into an efficient system.","To argue that Gutenberg stole his ideas from Asian inventors.","To show that European printing was inferior to Asian printing.","To explain why Gutenberg's Bible was printed in Latin."],0,"The author states that 'Gutenberg's achievement lay in combining them into a process efficient enough to change a continent.'"],["Which detail best supports the claim that printing changed science?",["Gutenberg adapted a press used for grapes and olives.","Luther called printing one of God's greatest gifts.","Scholars could share identical diagrams and correct errors in later editions.","About 180 copies of the Gutenberg Bible were printed."],2,"The science paragraph explains that identical diagrams let scholars 'check one another's work, build on it, and correct it.'"],["What does the author imply in the final paragraph about modern media such as the internet?",["They have solved the problems that printing created.","They pose the same challenge of deciding which information to trust.","They are less influential than the printing press was.","They should be controlled by authorities to prevent falsehoods."],1,"The author says every new medium 'has returned Europe's fifteenth-century dilemma to a new generation,' leaving readers to 'weigh, question, and compare.'"]]},{"id":"r9_4","lv":9,"e":"🛩️","t":"Queen Bess Takes the Sky","p":["In the early 1920s, crowds at American air shows grew used to a certain kind of daredevil: a young man in goggles and a leather jacket, looping and diving above a field of astonished farmers. Few of those spectators expected the pilot climbing into the cockpit to be a Black woman from rural Texas. Bessie Coleman meant to surprise them, and she intended to keep surprising them until the surprise wore off.","Coleman was born in 1892 in Atlanta, Texas, one of many children in a family that worked the cotton fields. She walked miles to a one-room segregated school, where she proved to be an eager student, especially in mathematics. School was regularly interrupted by harvest season, when every pair of hands was needed in the fields. She saved her earnings and eventually enrolled at a college in Oklahoma, but her money ran out after a single term.","Around 1915 she moved to Chicago, where two of her brothers lived, and found work as a manicurist. It was there, according to her own accounts, that her ambition took shape. Her brothers returned from military service in the First World War with stories about France, including the claim that French women flew airplanes. One brother teased her that she would never do anything so bold. The taunt, it seems, was precisely the wrong thing to say to Bessie Coleman.","Wanting to fly was one thing; finding someone willing to teach her was another. American flight schools refused to admit her, because she was both Black and a woman. Rather than surrender the idea, she took the advice of Robert Abbott, the publisher of the Chicago Defender, an influential Black newspaper, who suggested she go to France. She studied French, gathered money from her work and from supporters, and sailed for Europe in 1920.","At a flight school in northern France, she trained in fragile biplanes built of wood and fabric. In June 1921 she earned a pilot's license from the Fédération Aéronautique Internationale, making her the first African American woman, and the first woman of Native American descent, to hold a pilot's license. She had achieved abroad what she had been forbidden even to attempt at home.","Returning to the United States, Coleman discovered that a license alone did not pay the bills. Commercial aviation was still in its infancy, and for a pilot like her, the realistic career was exhibition flying, known as barnstorming. After additional training in Europe to master stunts, she began performing figure eights, loops, and daring dives before paying crowds. The press called her 'Queen Bess.'","She used her fame deliberately. On at least one well-known occasion in her home state of Texas, she refused to perform unless Black and white spectators could enter through the same gate. She also lectured at schools and churches, urging young Black Americans to consider careers in the air. Her larger dream was to open a flight school of her own, so that the next person with her ambitions would not have to cross an ocean to pursue them.","That dream remained unfulfilled. In 1926, during a practice flight in Florida before a scheduled exhibition, Coleman was killed in an accident. She was thirty-four years old. Thousands attended memorial services in her honor.","Her influence, however, outlasted her brief career. In 1929, aviators in Los Angeles founded a flying club named for her, and it helped train a new generation of Black pilots. Decades later, the United States Postal Service placed her image on a stamp. The school she imagined was built, in a sense, by the people she inspired. Bessie Coleman once said that the air was the only place free from prejudice; she spent her short life making the ground a little more like the sky."],"q":[["Why does the author open the biography by describing typical air-show pilots of the 1920s?",["To argue that most early pilots were reckless.","To explain how airplanes of the 1920s were built.","To set up a contrast that highlights how unexpected Coleman was.","To show that farmers disliked air shows."],2,"The opening describes 'a young man in goggles' and then notes that few 'expected the pilot... to be a Black woman from rural Texas.'"],["What does the author suggest by calling her brother's taunt 'precisely the wrong thing to say to Bessie Coleman'?",["The teasing strengthened her determination instead of discouraging her.","Her brother later apologized for insulting her.","She stopped speaking to her brothers after the argument.","She believed that French women were better pilots than men."],0,"The ironic phrasing follows the teasing that 'she would never do anything so bold,' and the rest of the passage shows her doing exactly that."],["Which detail best supports the claim that Coleman 'used her fame deliberately'?",["She trained in biplanes built of wood and fabric.","She refused to perform unless Black and white spectators could use the same gate.","The press gave her the nickname 'Queen Bess.'","She worked as a manicurist in Chicago."],1,"The paragraph beginning 'She used her fame deliberately' cites her refusal to perform at a segregated entrance."],["Based on the passage, why did Coleman go to France to learn to fly?",["French planes were safer than American ones.","Her brothers had arranged a job for her there.","She wanted to fly in the First World War.","American flight schools would not admit a Black woman."],3,"The passage states 'American flight schools refused to admit her, because she was both Black and a woman.'"],["What is the meaning of the final sentence's image of 'making the ground a little more like the sky'?",["She hoped to build airports in every American city.","She believed people should spend more time outdoors.","She worked to bring the freedom from prejudice she felt in the air to life on the ground.","She wanted to prove that flying was safer than driving."],2,"It echoes her statement that the air was 'the only place free from prejudice.'"]]},{"id":"r9_5","lv":9,"e":"🏞️","t":"Love the Parks Less Crowded","p":["Picture the moment you have seen in a hundred photographs: a hiker alone on a granite ledge, a canyon glowing below, silence stretching to the horizon. Now picture what many visitors actually find at the most famous overlooks on a summer afternoon: a parking lot full by nine, a line for the trailhead shuttle, and a crowd of phones held aloft at the same view. America's most beloved national parks are being loved to exhaustion. The most popular of them should limit the number of daily visitors through a fair reservation system.","The numbers tell the story. The national park system now records hundreds of millions of visits a year, and a handful of iconic parks absorb a disproportionate share. Roads designed decades ago for a fraction of today's traffic become gridlocked. Trails widen as hikers step around one another, trampling fragile plants. Wildlife, pressed by noise and constant human presence, changes its habits. Rangers spend their days directing traffic rather than teaching visitors. Even the experience visitors came for suffers: it is difficult to feel the grandeur of a waterfall while waiting forty minutes for a parking space within sight of it, listening to engines idle and horns complain.","The law that created the National Park Service in 1916 gave it a dual mission: to allow people to enjoy these places while leaving them 'unimpaired for the enjoyment of future generations.' When enjoyment today begins to impair the parks for tomorrow, the second half of that promise must carry weight. A park overwhelmed beyond its capacity is not being preserved; it is being slowly spent.","Reservations are not a radical experiment. Several popular parks have already tested timed-entry systems during peak seasons, and visitors have reported less congested roads and trails. Many campgrounds and backcountry permits have required reservations for years without anyone suggesting that the wilderness had been locked away.","Opponents make a serious objection: the parks belong to everyone, and a reservation system could favor people with fast internet connections, flexible jobs, and the ability to plan months ahead. A family that decides on a trip at the last minute, or a local resident who has visited freely for decades, might find the gate effectively closed. This is a real risk, and it should not be waved away.","But the answer to an unfair system is a fairer system, not no system at all. Parks can hold back a portion of entries for same-day release, set aside passes for nearby communities, run lotteries rather than first-come races, and offer entry by shuttle to visitors without reservations. Moreover, the current arrangement is hardly equal. Access now goes to whoever can arrive earliest and endure the longest lines, which quietly favors those with the most free time. Unmanaged crowding is not the absence of rationing; it is rationing by exhaustion.","Some also argue that limits will hurt the gateway towns whose restaurants and motels depend on park tourism. Yet predictable, spread-out visitation may serve these businesses better than chaotic summer peaks followed by empty months. A reservation system can encourage visits in spring and fall, smoothing the flow of customers rather than cutting it off.","None of this requires loving the parks less. It requires loving them wisely, in the way one cares for anything precious and finite. Wallace Stegner once called the national parks the best idea America ever had. An idea that good deserves protection from its own popularity. If we want our grandchildren to stand on that granite ledge and hear the silence, we must be willing, now and then, to wait our turn."],"q":[["Which statement best expresses the author's central claim?",["National parks should be closed to protect wildlife.","The most popular national parks should limit daily visitors through a fair reservation system.","Visitors should stop taking photographs in national parks.","Gateway towns should pay for park maintenance."],1,"The first paragraph ends with the thesis that the most popular parks 'should limit the number of daily visitors through a fair reservation system.'"],["What is the purpose of the contrast in the opening paragraph between the photograph and the real overlook?",["To expose the gap between the parks' ideal image and their crowded reality.","To argue that photographs of parks are dishonest advertising.","To suggest that hikers should visit only in winter.","To show that the author prefers photography to hiking."],0,"The author sets a 'hiker alone' against a lot 'full by nine' and 'a crowd of phones' to dramatize the overcrowding problem."],["How does the author respond to the objection that reservations could favor certain people?",["By arguing that fairness does not matter in public lands.","By claiming that the objection has no basis at all.","By conceding the risk and proposing ways to design a fairer system.","By suggesting that only local residents should be admitted."],2,"The author calls it 'a real risk' and then says 'the answer to an unfair system is a fairer system,' listing lotteries and same-day passes."],["What does the phrase 'rationing by exhaustion' imply about the current situation?",["Parks already require visitors to pass fitness tests.","Rangers are too tired to enforce park rules.","Visitors leave early because the trails are too difficult.","Access is already limited, but unfairly, by who can endure crowds and lines."],3,"The author argues access 'goes to whoever can arrive earliest and endure the longest lines,' so crowding is itself a form of rationing."],["Why does the author quote the 1916 law's phrase 'unimpaired for the enjoyment of future generations'?",["To show that the law requires parks to charge entrance fees.","To ground the argument in the parks' founding mission to preserve them for the future.","To prove that the Park Service has always used reservations.","To argue that the 1916 law is outdated and should be replaced."],1,"The author says that when enjoyment 'begins to impair the parks for tomorrow, the second half of that promise must carry weight.'"]]},{"id":"r9_6","lv":9,"e":"✈️","t":"The List That Saved the Bomber","p":["On October 30, 1935, a crowd of military officials gathered at Wright Field in Dayton, Ohio, to watch a demonstration flight of the Boeing Model 299. The aircraft was enormous for its time, a four-engine bomber that could fly farther and carry more than any competitor. Newspapers had nicknamed it a 'flying fortress.' The Army Air Corps was considering a large order, and the flight was expected to be a formality, little more than a ceremony before the contract was signed.","The plane rolled down the runway, climbed steeply, stalled, and crashed. Two of the crew, including the pilot, an experienced Army test pilot, died of their injuries. Investigators soon determined that nothing had been wrong with the aircraft's design. The crew had forgotten to release a mechanism that locked the elevator and rudder controls while the plane sat on the ground. With the controls locked, the pilot could not bring the nose down.","The conclusion was unsettling precisely because it was so ordinary. The pilot was among the most skilled in the service. He had not been careless in any obvious sense. The problem was that the new airplane demanded more steps than earlier aircraft, from managing four engines to adjusting propellers and flaps, and a capable human mind, under pressure, had simply skipped one. Some observers declared the airplane 'too much airplane for one man to fly.'","The Army did not give up on the design. Instead, a group of test pilots arrived at a remarkably humble solution. They wrote a checklist: a short card listing the essential steps for takeoff, flight, landing, and taxiing. The steps themselves were not new. Every pilot already knew them. What was new was the admission that knowing something and reliably doing it, every single time, are different skills.","With the checklist in use, the aircraft went on to fly many thousands of miles without a serious accident in testing. The Army ultimately ordered the bomber in large numbers, and it became the B-17, one of the most famous aircraft of the Second World War. The checklist, meanwhile, became a permanent fixture of aviation. What had begun as a single index card for one troubled airplane grew into thick binders and, eventually, electronic displays built into the instrument panels of modern jets.","Today, airline crews use checklists for nearly every phase of flight. Typically, one pilot reads each item aloud and the other confirms that it has been completed, a method sometimes called 'challenge and response.' This structure turns a private memory task into a shared, spoken routine, so that a single lapse is more likely to be caught by a second person. Separate checklists cover emergencies such as engine failures or fires, when stress makes clear thinking hardest and a printed sequence can steady a crew.","Pilots sometimes admit that checklists can feel tedious, even faintly insulting, to a professional who has performed the same steps a thousand times. Yet that feeling is exactly the danger the Model 299 exposed. Familiarity breeds confidence, and confidence can quietly erase a step. The checklist does not assume that pilots are foolish. It assumes they are human.","In recent decades, the idea has migrated well beyond the cockpit. Hospitals, construction firms, and other fields where small oversights can have serious consequences have borrowed the aviation checklist, sometimes with striking results. The lesson traveled because it was never really about airplanes. It was about the limits of memory, and about the unusual courage it takes for experts to write down what they already know."],"q":[["What is the central idea of the passage?",["The Boeing Model 299 was poorly designed and dangerous.","Only the most experienced pilots should fly large aircraft.","Checklists guard against the human tendency to skip steps, even among experts.","Military aircraft are more complicated than hospital equipment."],2,"The passage shows a skilled pilot missing one step and concludes that checklists assume pilots 'are human.'"],["Why does the author describe the crash's cause as unsettling 'precisely because it was so ordinary'?",["Because the crash showed that even highly skilled people can make simple mistakes.","Because crashes were common at Wright Field in the 1930s.","Because investigators could not agree on what went wrong.","Because the crowd expected the flight to be dangerous."],0,"The next sentences stress the pilot's skill and that 'a capable human mind, under pressure, had simply skipped one' step."],["According to the passage, what was genuinely new about the checklist?",["It taught pilots steps they had never learned before.","It replaced the need for a second pilot in the cockpit.","It was designed to be used only in emergencies.","It recognized that knowing a procedure differs from reliably performing it."],3,"The author writes that 'every pilot already knew' the steps; the new idea was that 'knowing something and reliably doing it... are different skills.'"],["What is the purpose of the 'challenge and response' method described in the passage?",["To test whether a pilot has memorized the checklist.","To make memory a shared task so one person's lapse can be caught by another.","To speed up takeoff by dividing the work in half.","To allow air traffic controllers to monitor the crew."],1,"The passage says it 'turns a private memory task into a shared, spoken routine, so that a single lapse is more likely to be caught.'"],["What does the author imply by ending with 'the unusual courage it takes for experts to write down what they already know'?",["Experts often refuse to share their knowledge with beginners.","Writing checklists is physically dangerous work.","Admitting one's fallibility, even as an expert, requires humility and courage.","Experts usually know less than they claim to know."],2,"The ending echoes the 'remarkably humble solution' and the idea that checklists can feel 'faintly insulting' to professionals."]]},{"id":"r10_1","lv":10,"e":"🕰️","t":"The Inventory","p":["The bell above the door of Halloran Watch Repair still worked, which struck Nadia as a small act of defiance. Her grandfather had been dead for five weeks, the lease expired at the end of the month, and yet the bell announced her arrival with the same bright, officious ring it had given every customer for forty years, as though nothing in the world had been settled.","Her brother Colm was already inside, standing behind the counter with a clipboard. He had driven down from the city that morning in a pressed shirt, and he held the clipboard the way other people hold a shield. 'I've started the inventory,' he said. 'The dealer comes Thursday. He'll take the lathes and the good tools as a lot, and he says the clocks on the back wall are worth more than we'd guess.'","'You called a dealer,' Nadia said. It was not quite a question.","'Someone had to.' He did not look up. 'You were going to spend three weeks in here holding each screwdriver up to the light and remembering things. I understand that. I just don't have three weeks.'","She let the remark pass, partly because it was fair. She had, in fact, planned to spend three weeks in the shop. She had told her supervisor at the school that she needed time for family matters, and she had imagined those days in some detail: the radio tuned low, the smell of oil and brass, the slow work of wrapping each object in newspaper while she decided what to keep. She had not imagined Colm, or the clipboard, or Thursday.","She drifted toward the workbench, where their grandfather's loupe still lay beside a pocket watch with its back removed. The movement inside was exposed, a tiny city of wheels halted mid-thought. Someone had brought it in for cleaning; a paper tag bore the name Esposito and a phone number in her grandfather's cramped hand. 'This isn't ours,' she said. 'It belongs to a customer.'","Colm glanced over. 'Put it in the box with the others. There's a box of customer pieces under the register. I was going to call them all.' He hesitated, then added, more quietly, 'There are sixty-one of them. I counted last night.'","Nadia turned. 'Last night?'","'I got in around ten. Couldn't sleep at the motel.' He shrugged, as if insomnia were a scheduling inconvenience. 'Sixty-one watches, eleven clocks, two music boxes. Most of them he'd already fixed. People just never came back for them. Some of the tags are from the nineties.' He set the clipboard down for the first time. 'He kept every one. He wrote on the tags when he tried to reach them. Called twice, no answer. Letter returned. Called again. One of them has seven dates on it.'","Nadia found she had nothing to say. She had spent the drive assembling a version of her brother: brisk, impatient, eager to convert the last room of their childhood into a line on a spreadsheet. The version had been comfortable, because it allowed her to be the one who grieved properly. Now she looked at the clipboard he had put down and saw, beneath the columns of lathes and lots, a second list in smaller writing, a list of names and numbers, several already checked.","'You've been calling them,' she said.","'Nine so far. Mrs. Esposito's is on there. Her husband died in the spring. She cried on the phone and then apologized for crying, which was worse.' He picked up a brass key from the bench and turned it over without looking at it. 'I thought if the dealer took everything else, fine. But not these. These were never ours to sell.'","The bell above the door stayed silent, for once. Outside, a delivery truck idled, then pulled away. Nadia picked up the pocket watch, closed its case with a soft click, and set it carefully in the box beneath the register, among the sixty others still waiting to be claimed.","'Give me half the list,' she said. 'I'll take the ones from the nineties. I have more patience for disappointment than you do.'","Colm almost smiled. He tore the page along its fold and handed her the longer half."],"q":[["Which choice best describes a central development in the passage?",["Nadia revises a convenient but mistaken judgment of her brother after discovering what he has been doing.","Nadia persuades Colm to cancel the dealer's visit and keep the shop open.","Colm admits that he regrets having left the family business for the city.","Nadia and Colm argue over which of them their grandfather loved more."],0,"Nadia had 'assembled a version of her brother' as brisk and impatient, but the list of customer names shows he has been quietly calling each owner."],["The narrator's description of Colm holding the clipboard 'the way other people hold a shield' most strongly suggests that he",["is proud of how quickly he has organized the inventory","wants Nadia to admire his efficiency","uses practical tasks to protect himself from emotion","is hiding the clipboard's contents from the dealer"],2,"The shield image implies defense, which fits later details such as his sleeplessness and his distress over Mrs. Esposito's call."],["As used in the passage, the phrase 'a tiny city of wheels halted mid-thought' mainly serves to",["emphasize the watch's monetary value to the dealer","suggest an interrupted life and unfinished work, echoing the grandfather's death","show that Nadia understands watch repair as well as her grandfather did","criticize the customer for abandoning the watch"],1,"The stopped movement on the bench, with its back still removed, mirrors work left unfinished when the grandfather died five weeks earlier."],["The detail that one tag 'has seven dates on it' primarily reveals",["that the grandfather's business was poorly managed","that customers in the nineties were careless with valuables","that Colm distrusts his grandfather's record-keeping","the grandfather's persistent sense of obligation to his customers"],3,"Colm notes the grandfather 'kept every one' and wrote each attempt to reach the owners on the tags, showing his dogged loyalty to them."],["Nadia's final remark that she has 'more patience for disappointment' than Colm most likely indicates that she",["believes most customers will be pleased to hear from them","plans to leave the calls unfinished","is offering, with gentle humor, to take on the harder share of the work","resents Colm for giving her the longer half of the list"],2,"She asks for the oldest tags, those least likely to reach anyone, and Colm 'almost smiled,' marking a softened, cooperative tone."]]},{"id":"r10_2","lv":10,"e":"🐜","t":"The Ant That Counts Its Steps","p":["The salt flats of North Africa are among the least forgiving places an insect can choose to live. The ground is nearly featureless, the surface temperature can exceed the point at which most animal proteins begin to fail, and food, usually the bodies of other insects killed by the heat, is scattered at random. Yet a desert ant of the genus Cataglyphis will leave its nest, travel a looping, meandering path of a hundred meters or more in search of such a meal, and then, having found one, turn and run home in a nearly straight line. The return is so direct that it seems to demand an explanation. An ant that wandered home as aimlessly as it had wandered out would spend so long on the scorching surface that it might never arrive at all, so the precision of that homeward sprint is not a curiosity but a condition of survival.","Part of the explanation had been established for decades. These ants carry a kind of celestial compass: specialized cells in their eyes detect the pattern of polarized light in the sky, allowing them to hold a heading relative to the sun even when the sun itself is obscured. A compass, however, answers only half the question. To travel home in a straight line, an animal must know not only which direction to go but how far. Direction without distance is like a map with an arrow and no scale.","How, then, does an ant measure distance? One hypothesis held that it relied on optic flow, the rate at which the ground seems to stream past the eyes. Another proposed that the ant simply kept track of time spent walking. A third, which struck some researchers as almost too mechanical to be true, suggested that the ant counted, or at least integrated, its own steps, functioning as a living pedometer. Each hypothesis made a different prediction, and that difference is what made an experiment possible.","To distinguish among these possibilities, a team of researchers trained ants to walk through a straight channel from their nest to a feeder ten meters away. Once the ants had learned the route, the experimenters captured some of them at the feeder and altered their legs. In one group, tiny extensions made of pig bristle were glued to each leg, effectively placing the ants on stilts and lengthening their stride. In another group, the lower segments of the legs were trimmed, shortening their stride. A third group was left unaltered as a control. The ants were then released into a parallel test channel and observed as they searched for the nest.","The results were strikingly orderly. Unaltered ants began searching for the nest at roughly the correct distance. Ants on stilts overshot it, beginning their search well past the ten-meter mark, while ants with shortened legs stopped short. Crucially, the magnitude of the error roughly matched the change in stride length. If an ant's stride had been lengthened by a certain proportion, it traveled approximately that proportion farther before concluding it had arrived.","The researchers then added a second, more subtle test. Ants whose legs had been altered were allowed to make a full round trip, walking outbound to the feeder and back home on their new legs. When these ants were subsequently tested, they judged the distance accurately. Because they had covered both legs of the journey with the same altered stride, the count going out matched the count coming back.","This second result is what gives the experiment its persuasive force. The first test alone might be explained in other ways; perhaps the modified ants were simply disoriented or physically impaired. But impairment would not neatly correct itself after a single outbound trip. The pattern makes sense only if the ant's sense of distance is built from some internal record of stepping, calibrated by the stride it happens to be using.","The findings do not mean that optic flow plays no role at all; later work suggests that ants, like many navigators, combine several sources of information and weigh them according to circumstance. Nor does 'counting' imply anything like arithmetic. What the experiment reveals is humbler and, perhaps, more remarkable: that a brain smaller than a grain of sand can maintain a running tally of its own movement, fuse it with a compass reading drawn from the sky, and compute a route home across a blank and burning plain, and do it again, reliably, every time it ventures out."],"q":[["The primary purpose of the passage is to",["argue that optic flow is irrelevant to insect navigation","compare the navigational abilities of ants with those of other desert animals","describe how the ants' sky compass was first discovered","explain an experiment that helped identify how desert ants judge distance"],3,"Most of the passage details the stilts-and-stumps experiment and what it revealed about how ants measure 'how far.'"],["The comparison of 'a map with an arrow and no scale' in the second paragraph serves mainly to",["illustrate why a compass alone is insufficient for a direct return home","suggest that ants are poor at reading directional cues","show that researchers had misunderstood the polarized-light compass","introduce the idea that ants measure time rather than distance"],0,"The analogy follows the claim that 'direction without distance' answers only half the question."],["Based on the passage, ants with shortened legs that were released immediately after capture would most likely",["search for the nest at exactly the correct distance","overshoot the nest by a large margin","begin searching before reaching the nest's true location","refuse to leave the feeder"],2,"The passage states that 'ants with shortened legs stopped short.'"],["The author suggests that the round-trip test is especially important because it",["proved that ants use optic flow as their main cue","rules out the explanation that altered ants were merely impaired or disoriented","showed that stilts improved the ants' overall speed","demonstrated that unaltered ants make frequent errors"],1,"The author notes that 'impairment would not neatly correct itself after a single outbound trip,' so the corrected result supports step integration."],["In the final paragraph, the author's attitude toward the findings is best described as",["measured admiration","reluctant skepticism","detached indifference","unqualified triumph"],0,"The author adds caveats ('do not mean that optic flow plays no role') yet calls the result 'humbler and, perhaps, more remarkable.'"]]},{"id":"r10_3","lv":10,"e":"🔁","t":"The Window of the Unfamiliar","p":["Every January, millions of people resolve to exercise more, eat better, or spend less, and by February a large share of them have quietly returned to their old routines. The standard interpretation of this pattern is moral: people lack willpower, or they did not want the change badly enough. Behavioral researchers have increasingly argued that this interpretation misreads the problem. Much of what we do each day is not chosen at all, at least not in the deliberate sense that resolutions assume. It is triggered.","A habit, in the psychological sense, is not simply a frequent behavior. It is a behavior that has become linked to a context, such as a place, a time of day, a preceding action, or the presence of certain people, so tightly that the context alone is enough to set it in motion. The morning coffee is prompted less by a decision than by the kitchen, the hour, and the sight of the mug. Studies in which participants record their activities throughout the day have found that a substantial portion of everyday behavior is repeated in the same location nearly every day, and that people are often thinking about something else entirely while performing it.","This automaticity is not a flaw. It is, in fact, an efficiency. A mind that had to deliberate over every step of brushing its teeth would have little attention left for anything else. The difficulty is that the same mechanism that frees us from deliberation also shields established behaviors from our intentions. When the cue appears, the routine runs, regardless of what we resolved on New Year's Eve. Intentions, in the words of one researcher, tend to predict behavior well only when habits are weak.","If this account is correct, it implies something that sounds paradoxical: the best time to change a habit may be when life is disrupted. When the cues that sustain a routine disappear, the routine loses its automatic trigger, and for a while behavior must once again be governed by conscious choice. Researchers have called this idea the habit discontinuity hypothesis.","Evidence for it has come from studies of people undergoing natural transitions. In one well-known line of research, investigators surveyed people about their commuting habits and their environmental values, then followed up after some of them had moved to a new city. Among those who had not moved, strong environmental values had relatively little connection to how they traveled to work; most simply continued doing what they had always done. Among recent movers, however, the relationship was noticeably stronger. People who cared about the environment were more likely to adopt public transit or cycling in their new location. The move, it seems, opened a window in which values could finally reach behavior.","The findings carry practical implications for anyone who designs programs meant to change behavior. A public health campaign encouraging exercise may be wasted on people deep in stable routines yet unusually effective when directed at new employees, new parents, or students arriving on campus. Some cities have experimented with offering free transit passes to new residents rather than to the population as a whole, reasoning that the same money yields a larger effect when it arrives at a moment of flux.","Still, the window is not an unmixed opportunity. Discontinuity strips away good habits as readily as bad ones. The runner who moves across the country may find that the loss of a familiar route and a regular partner quietly ends years of exercise. Disruption does not favor improvement; it merely makes behavior temporarily negotiable again. What fills the gap depends on what the person, or the environment, supplies.","For individuals, the lesson is less about summoning heroic willpower than about paying attention to timing and context. A resolution made in the middle of an unchanged life asks intention to fight against every familiar cue. A resolution made during a transition, or one that deliberately alters the cues themselves, by moving the running shoes to the doorway, for example, or by leaving the phone in another room, enlists the environment as an ally rather than an adversary. Change, on this view, is less a matter of trying harder than of choosing the moment, and arranging the setting, in which trying can actually matter."],"q":[["The author mentions the 'standard interpretation' in the first paragraph primarily in order to",["endorse the view that resolutions fail from lack of desire","introduce a common explanation that the passage goes on to challenge","summarize findings from studies of recent movers","argue that January is the worst month to begin a habit"],1,"The author says researchers argue this moral interpretation 'misreads the problem' and then offers the cue-based account."],["As used in the third paragraph, 'shields' most nearly means",["decorates","hides from view","rescues","protects against interference"],3,"The automatic mechanism keeps established routines from being altered by 'our intentions,' so it protects them from interference."],["According to the passage, the commuting study found that environmental values were more closely connected to travel behavior among",["people who had recently moved","people with the strongest environmental values regardless of moving","long-term residents who used public transit","people who had never owned a car"],0,"'Among recent movers, however, the relationship was noticeably stronger.'"],["The seventh paragraph (beginning 'Still, the window') functions mainly to",["reject the habit discontinuity hypothesis as unsupported","provide further statistical evidence for the commuting study","qualify the preceding argument by noting that disruption can also end good habits","suggest that runners are especially vulnerable to relocation"],2,"The paragraph notes that 'discontinuity strips away good habits as readily as bad ones,' tempering the optimism before it."],["The author would most likely agree with which statement?",["Habits are harmful and should be replaced with conscious decisions whenever possible.","Willpower is irrelevant to behavior under all circumstances.","Public programs should target the entire population equally at all times.","Altering one's surroundings can make an intended change more likely to last."],3,"The final paragraph recommends changing cues, like moving the shoes to the doorway, so the environment becomes 'an ally rather than an adversary.'"]]},{"id":"r10_4","lv":10,"e":"⚖️","t":"Ambition Against Ambition","p":["When the framers of the United States Constitution gathered in Philadelphia in the summer of 1787, they faced a problem that had troubled political thinkers for centuries. A government strong enough to protect liberty is, by that very strength, capable of destroying it. The Articles of Confederation had erred on the side of weakness, leaving the national government unable to raise revenue or settle disputes among the states. Yet many delegates remembered vividly the abuses of a distant king and feared that a vigorous central authority would simply reproduce them under a new name. The delegates, in other words, had to design a government that was powerful enough to govern and yet somehow incapable of becoming what they had fought a war to escape.","The solution they adopted drew heavily on an idea that the French writer Montesquieu had popularized several decades earlier. In his survey of the world's governments, Montesquieu argued that liberty depends on dividing the power to make laws, the power to carry them out, and the power to judge disputes under them. When all three are united in a single person or body, he warned, no citizen can feel secure, because the same hands that write an oppressive law can enforce it and then approve their own conduct.","Separating these functions on paper, however, was not enough. A constitution might announce that the legislature shall not interfere with the courts, but such announcements are, in the phrase the framers sometimes used, mere 'parchment barriers.' What would prevent an ambitious branch from simply ignoring the text? Some state constitutions of the period had declared their branches separate, only to watch the legislatures absorb nearly every important power within a few years.","James Madison, writing in defense of the proposed Constitution, offered an answer notable for its lack of sentimentality. He did not rely on the virtue of officeholders. Instead, he proposed to give each branch both the constitutional means and the personal motive to resist encroachment by the others. The interests of the individual official, he reasoned, should be connected to the constitutional rights of the office. A senator jealous of the Senate's authority, a president protective of the executive's prerogatives, and judges secure in their tenure would each defend their own ground, and in doing so would keep the others in their places. The system would not depend on anyone's goodness; it would depend on everyone's self-interest. Ambition, in short, would be set against ambition.","Madison was frank about the view of human nature this design implied. If people were angels, he observed, no government would be needed at all; if angels governed people, no controls on government would be necessary. Because neither condition holds, a government must first be able to control the governed and then be obliged to control itself. Elections, which make officials dependent on the people, were the primary safeguard. But experience, he argued, had taught the need for what he called auxiliary precautions.","The resulting system was deliberately untidy. Rather than a clean separation, the Constitution produced an overlapping arrangement in which each branch holds a partial share in the work of the others. The president may veto legislation; Congress may override that veto, approve or reject appointments, and remove officials through impeachment; judges, once appointed, serve during good behavior and cannot have their salaries reduced. These 'checks and balances' ensure that no branch can act entirely alone on matters of great consequence.","Critics then and since have pointed out that such a design is slow, prone to deadlock, and frustrating to anyone eager for decisive action. The framers were not unaware of this cost. To many of them, a certain amount of friction was precisely the point. A government that found it difficult to act would also find it difficult to act tyrannically, and legislation that survived the scrutiny of several independent bodies was more likely to reflect a broad consensus than a passing majority's impulse. Moreover, the delays imposed by divided power offered time for passions to cool and for the public to reconsider.","Whether that trade has been wise remains a live question, and reasonable people weigh the balance between energy and restraint differently in different eras. What is striking, looking back, is the modesty of the framers' underlying premise. They did not assume that good people would always hold office. They built a structure meant to function even when they did not."],"q":[["Which choice best states the central idea of the passage?",["The framers believed elected officials would be virtuous enough to restrain themselves.","Montesquieu designed the American system of checks and balances.","The framers created overlapping powers that harness officials' self-interest to guard against tyranny.","The Articles of Confederation failed because they gave the states too little power."],2,"The passage centers on Madison's plan to give each branch 'the constitutional means and the personal motive' to resist the others."],["The reference to state constitutions in the third paragraph serves mainly to",["show that the framers copied existing state governments","provide evidence that written separations alone could fail","argue that legislatures should hold most governmental power","illustrate Montesquieu's influence on local politics"],1,"Those states 'declared their branches separate, only to watch the legislatures absorb nearly every important power,' illustrating 'parchment barriers.'"],["As used in the fourth paragraph, 'jealous of' most nearly means",["envious of","suspicious of","resentful toward","vigilantly protective of"],3,"The senator 'jealous of the Senate's authority' is grouped with officials who 'defend their own ground,' meaning guarding it closely."],["Madison's remark about angels, as paraphrased in the passage, primarily supports which idea?",["Because people are imperfect, government needs internal controls on itself as well as power over the governed.","Elections are unnecessary if officials are chosen carefully.","Religious principles should guide the design of government.","The people cannot be trusted to choose their leaders."],0,"The passage concludes from the angel remark that 'a government must first be able to control the governed and then be obliged to control itself.'"],["The author's attitude toward the framers' design in the final two paragraphs is best described as",["dismissive of its inefficiency","appreciative of its realism while acknowledging its costs remain debated","uncritically celebratory of its speed","nostalgic for the Articles of Confederation"],1,"The author admits the trade 'remains a live question' but calls 'striking' the 'modesty of the framers' underlying premise.'"]]},{"id":"r10_5","lv":10,"e":"🛩️","t":"Volunteers of the Sky","p":["In the first days of December 1941, before the attack on Pearl Harbor pulled the United States fully into the Second World War, a group of civilian pilots persuaded the federal government to accept an unusual offer. They owned small airplanes, they knew how to fly them, and they wanted to put both to use in the nation's defense. The organization formed that week, the Civil Air Patrol, was built on a premise that still defines it: that ordinary citizens, freely volunteering their time and skill, can take on work that matters.","The work mattered almost at once. Enemy submarines were sinking merchant ships within sight of the American coast, and the military did not yet have enough aircraft to watch the shipping lanes. Civil Air Patrol pilots began flying patrols over the Atlantic and the Gulf of Mexico in light planes never designed for such duty. They reported submarines, guided rescuers to survivors of sinkings, and accepted real danger to do so; some of these volunteers did not come home. Their presence overhead, historians generally agree, made it harder for submarines to operate close to shore.","After the war, the organization might easily have faded, as many wartime improvisations did. Instead, Congress chartered it as a nonprofit corporation, and shortly afterward it was designated the official civilian auxiliary of the newly independent United States Air Force. That relationship continues. Members wear uniforms and follow a rank structure, yet they remain volunteers, generally unpaid, who fit their service around jobs, school, and families.","Today the Civil Air Patrol describes its purpose in terms of three missions. The first, emergency services, is the most visible. Aircrews and ground teams search for missing aircraft and lost people, support disaster relief, and photograph areas struck by floods, fires, or storms so that officials can see the extent of the damage. Much of the inland search and rescue in the United States involves the organization in some way, often at the request of the Air Force. The second mission, aerospace education, reaches both members and the wider public, offering teachers materials and experiences that bring flight, rocketry, and space science into classrooms. The third, the cadet program, is devoted to young people.","Cadets join as teenagers and may continue into early adulthood. What they encounter is less a club than a carefully designed ladder. Each rung, or achievement, asks something specific: a test of knowledge in leadership or aerospace, a physical fitness standard, participation in character discussions, and active involvement in the unit. Completing an achievement brings a promotion in cadet grade, and with it greater responsibility. Major milestones along the way carry the names of figures from aviation history, from the Wright brothers to Amelia Earhart, and the program's highest award is earned by only a small fraction of those who start.","The structure of this ladder reveals a particular philosophy of leadership. A new cadet begins by learning to follow: to wear the uniform correctly, to listen, to be where the team needs her. Only after mastering that stage is she asked to lead a small group, then to command larger ones, and eventually to plan and run activities that younger cadets depend on. The sequence is deliberate. It assumes that a person who has never been responsible for doing a task well has little standing to direct others in doing it, and that authority is best understood not as a reward for past effort but as an obligation toward the people one leads.","This idea echoes throughout the organization's adult work as well. On a search mission, the incident commander may be a retired airline captain or a schoolteacher; the observer in the right seat may be a college student. Rank matters, but so does training, and the person best qualified for a task is expected to perform it regardless of age or title. Leadership, in this setting, is less about standing at the front than about knowing when to step forward and when to support someone else.","It would be easy to romanticize such service, and the organization's own members tend to resist doing so. Much of the work is unglamorous: maintaining aircraft, filling out training records, standing in a parking lot at dawn waiting for a mission that may be called off. Yet that ordinariness is, in a sense, the point. The pilots who first volunteered in 1941 were not professional soldiers. They were citizens who noticed a need and decided that their skills obliged them to meet it. The Civil Air Patrol has spent the decades since trying to teach that same lesson to each new generation: that service is not an occasional heroic gesture but a steady willingness to be useful."],"q":[["Which choice best states the central idea of the passage?",["The Civil Air Patrol reflects a view of service and leadership rooted in citizens volunteering their skills and accepting responsibility for others.","The Civil Air Patrol was primarily a wartime organization whose importance ended in 1945.","The cadet program exists mainly to recruit future Air Force pilots.","Emergency services are the only mission that gives the Civil Air Patrol its value."],0,"The passage opens and closes with the premise that 'ordinary citizens, freely volunteering their time and skill, can take on work that matters.'"],["The second paragraph serves mainly to",["argue that light planes were better suited to coastal patrol than military aircraft","criticize the military for being unprepared in 1941","explain why the organization later became an Air Force auxiliary","show that the founders' offer of service quickly proved valuable and costly"],3,"It describes patrols that reported submarines and aided survivors, noting that 'some of these volunteers did not come home.'"],["As used in the fifth paragraph, 'rung' most nearly means",["a signal of alarm","a type of military rank insignia","a stage in a progression","a circle of close members"],2,"The author calls the program 'a carefully designed ladder,' and each rung is an 'achievement' leading to promotion."],["Based on the sixth paragraph, the author suggests that the cadet program treats authority primarily as",["a privilege earned by seniority alone","a responsibility owed to those one leads, built on first learning to follow","an honor reserved for cadets who win the highest award","a skill that cannot be taught but only discovered"],1,"The program assumes authority is 'an obligation toward the people one leads' and begins by teaching cadets 'to follow.'"],["In the final paragraph, the author acknowledges that much of the work is 'unglamorous' most likely in order to",["discourage readers from volunteering","suggest that the organization has lost its original purpose","reinforce the idea that true service lies in steady, ordinary usefulness","contrast modern members unfavorably with the 1941 pilots"],2,"The author says 'that ordinariness is, in a sense, the point' and defines service as 'a steady willingness to be useful.'"]]},{"id":"r10_6","lv":10,"e":"🧭","t":"In Praise of Getting Lost","p":["There is a particular silence that falls over a car when the navigation app loses its signal. Conversation stops. Someone holds a phone toward the windshield as though offering it to the sky. For a moment, the occupants confront a question that, a generation ago, would have seemed absurd to ask: where are we, actually? The road is the same road it was a minute earlier, yet no one in the car can say with any confidence where it leads. I want to suggest that this moment of disorientation, uncomfortable as it is, deserves more respect than we give it, and that a habit of never allowing it may cost us more than we realize.","Turn-by-turn navigation is one of the genuine marvels of modern life. It has spared countless travelers from wrong exits and missed appointments, and I have no wish to return to the era of creased paper maps spread across a steering wheel. But convenience has a way of concealing what it replaces. When a voice tells us to turn left in four hundred feet, we comply, and we arrive. What we do not do is build a picture of the place we have passed through. We have been delivered rather than traveled.","The distinction matters because spatial understanding appears to be something the mind develops through use. Researchers who study navigation have observed that people who plan their own routes tend to engage brain regions associated with mapping and memory more actively than people who follow step-by-step instructions. Studies of habitual users of navigation devices have reported, on average, weaker recall of the routes they traveled and less ability to sketch the layout of a neighborhood afterward. The evidence is not conclusive, and correlation can mislead; people with a poor sense of direction may simply rely on devices more. Still, the pattern is consistent enough to take seriously.","More is at stake than the ability to find a pharmacy without a phone. A mental map is also a relationship with a place. The person who has wandered a city, doubled back, noticed the bakery on the corner where she took a wrong turn, knows that city in a way the efficiently routed visitor does not. Getting lost, in small and safe doses, forces attention outward. It asks us to read street signs, the angle of the sun, the slope of a hill toward the river. It turns the world from a backdrop into a text.","The strongest objection to this argument deserves a fair hearing. For many people, getting lost is neither charming nor safe. A person with a visual impairment, an elderly driver in unfamiliar traffic, a newcomer who does not speak the local language, or someone walking alone at night may find that navigation technology is the difference between independence and isolation. To romanticize disorientation from a position of security is to ignore those for whom the stakes are real. Any defense of getting lost that does not reckon with this is a luxury masquerading as wisdom.","I accept the force of this objection, and it narrows my claim considerably. I am not arguing that anyone should abandon a tool that keeps them safe or free. I am arguing that those of us for whom a wrong turn is merely an inconvenience have begun to treat every inconvenience as an emergency, and that in doing so we have surrendered a capacity we did not know we valued. The question is not whether to use the device but whether to use it by default, reflexively, for trips we could manage on our own.","The remedy is modest. Glance at the map before leaving, then put the phone away and try to reach the destination from memory. Walk a neighborhood without a destination at all. Let a child navigate the route to a friend's house and tolerate the detour, answering its questions with questions of your own: which way is the river, and how do you know? None of this requires rejecting technology; it requires only the occasional decision to let the tool wait in a pocket.","We often speak of being lost as a failure, a gap between where we are and where we intended to be. But the gap is also where noticing happens. The traveler who never strays may arrive on time, but she arrives, in a sense, having been nowhere. Occasionally, it is worth the extra ten minutes to discover where we have been."],"q":[["The author's central claim is best summarized as which of the following?",["Navigation apps should be avoided because they are unreliable.","People who can navigate safely without devices should sometimes choose to, in order to preserve spatial understanding and attention to place.","Paper maps are superior to digital navigation in every respect.","Research has proven that navigation devices damage the brain."],1,"The author narrows the claim to 'those of us for whom a wrong turn is merely an inconvenience' and urges not using the device 'by default.'"],["The phrase 'We have been delivered rather than traveled' in the second paragraph most nearly suggests that app users",["reach their destinations passively, without engaging with the places they pass","arrive later than those who use paper maps","prefer public transportation to driving","are more likely to take wrong exits"],0,"The sentence follows the claim that app users do not 'build a picture of the place we have passed through.'"],["In the third paragraph, the author's acknowledgment that 'correlation can mislead' primarily serves to",["reject the research on navigation as worthless","show that people with poor direction should avoid devices","prove that navigation devices cause memory loss","present the evidence cautiously while still treating it as meaningful"],3,"The author concedes the evidence 'is not conclusive' but says the pattern 'is consistent enough to take seriously.'"],["How does the sixth paragraph relate to the fifth?",["It dismisses the objection as exaggerated.","It introduces a second, unrelated objection.","It concedes the objection's validity and narrows the scope of the argument in response.","It provides new scientific evidence that refutes the objection."],2,"The author writes, 'I accept the force of this objection, and it narrows my claim considerably.'"],["The author would most likely agree that the phrase 'a luxury masquerading as wisdom' describes",["a defense of getting lost that ignores people for whom disorientation is dangerous","the widespread use of navigation technology","the habit of glancing at a map before leaving","research on the brain regions involved in navigation"],0,"The author applies the phrase to 'any defense of getting lost that does not reckon with' those facing real risks, and accepts that criticism."]]}];
